{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01Swk6mZbvgC7yW4mebbyiHW","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_017kGSoqynMNK6vWAqStcPQx","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"Research MLE Rounds code path","subagent_type":"Explore","prompt":"I'm analyzing the GPU prover's MLE (Multi-Linear Extension) Rounds phase in the stark-backend repo. This phase shows REVERSE scaling: 114ms at APC 0 (99 AIRs) but 173ms at APC 300 (623 AIRs), despite the total work (cells, constraints) being 2x LESS at APC 300. I need to understand why.\n\nKey files:\n- `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/` — Rust side\n- `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/` — CUDA side\n\nSpecifically look for:\n1. The MLE sumcheck round loop — where rounds iterate\n2. Per-AIR overhead within each round (host-side work, kernel launches, D2H copies)\n3. How \"batched\" kernels work (previous optimizations added descriptor-array batched kernels for MLE)\n4. What remains per-AIR even after batching\n5. Any sequential host processing between rounds\n\nThe previous optimizations batch-mle-round-kernels and batch-mle-interpolation already batched some kernel launches. But per-round overhead is still ~8.7ms at APC 300 vs ~5.7ms at APC 0.\n\nReport: file paths, line numbers, the per-round code flow, and what per-AIR overhead remains. Be very thorough (200+ lines of context).","run_in_background":true},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4957,"cache_read_input_tokens":53889,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":4957},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a8941d98-8943-40ba-ba2b-e8ee56c821e0","uuid":"6e35bf39-6fbc-48b9-94a1-6f5e8919fac6"}
