   let width = mat.width();\n                        let output_height = height >> 1;\n                        max_output_cells = max(max_output_cells, output_height * width);\n                        let output_mat = DeviceMatrix::<EF>::with_capacity(output_height, width);\n                        (output_height.ilog2() as u8, width as u32, output_mat)\n                    })\n                    .multiunzip();\n\n            let input_ptrs = input_mats\n                .iter()\n                .take(num_matrices)\n                .map(|mat| mat.buffer().as_ptr())\n                .collect_vec();\n            let output_ptrs = output_mats\n                .iter()\n                .map(|mat| mat.buffer().as_mut_ptr())\n                .collect_vec();\n\n            let d_input_ptrs = input_ptrs.to_device()?;\n            let d_output_ptrs = output_ptrs.to_device()?;\n            let d_log_output_heights = log_output_heights.to_device()?;\n            let d_widths = widths.to_device()?;\n\n            unsafe {\n                batch_fold_mle(\n                    &d_input_ptrs,\n                    &d_output_ptrs,\n                    &d_widths,\n                    num_matrices.try_into().unwrap(),\n                    &d_log_output_heights,\n                    max_output_cells.try_into().unwrap(),\n                    r_round,\n                )\n                .map_err(LogupZerocheckError::BatchFoldMle)?;\n            }\n            output_mats.extend_from_slice(&input_mats[num_matrices..]);\n            Ok(output_mats)\n        };\n\n        // Fold mat_evals_per_trace: Vec<Vec<DeviceMatrix<EF>>>\n        self.mat_evals_per_trace = {\n            let lengths = self\n                .mat_evals_per_trace\n                .iter()\n                .map(|v| v.len())\n                .collect_vec();\n            let input_mats = std::mem::take(&mut self.mat_evals_per_trace)\n                .into_iter()\n                .flatten()\n                .collect_vec();\n            let mut output_mats = batch_fold(input_mats)?.into_iter();\n            lengths\n                .into_iter()\n                .map(|len| output_mats.by_ref().take(len).collect())\n                .collect()\n        };\n        if self.save_memory {\n            self.memory_limit_bytes = self.gkr_mem_contribution.saturating_sub(\n                self.mat_evals_per_trace\n                    .iter()\n                    .flatten()\n                    .map(|m| m.buffer().len() * size_of::<EF>())\n                    .sum(),\n            );\n        }\n\n        // Fold sels_per_trace: Vec<DeviceMatrix<EF>>\n        self.sels_per_trace = batch_fold(std::mem::take(&mut self.sels_per_trace))?;\n\n        for tree in self.eq_xis.values_mut() {\n            // trim the back (which corresponds to r_{j-1}) because we don't need it anymore\n            if tree.layers.len() > 1 {\n                tree.layers.pop();\n            }\n        }\n        let xi = self.xi[self.l_skip + round - 1];\n        let eq_r = eval_eq_mle(&[xi], &[r_round]);\n        self.eq_ns.push(self.eq_ns[round - 1] * eq_r);\n        self.eq_sharp_ns.push(self.eq_sharp_ns[round - 1] * eq_r);\n        Ok(())\n    }\n\n    #[instrument(\n        name = \"LogupZerocheck::into_column_openings\",\n        level = \"debug\",\n        skip_all\n    )]","numLines":100,"startLine":1998,"totalLines":2197}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01K2jVRD7QtojPaPkQz1U1xc","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I see `fold_mle_evals`:\n\n1. Flattens all per-trace matrices into one big list\n2. Calls `batch_fold_mle` — a single batched kernel that halves the height of all matrices\n3. Splits the results back into per-trace groups\n4. Also folds sels_per_trace (another `batch_fold`)\n5. Trims eq_xi layers\n\nThis is already reasonably batched. The per-trace overhead here is:\n- Creating `DeviceMatrix::with_capacity` for each output matrix (calls cudaMallocAsync)\n\n\n- Collecting input/output pointers and doing small H2D uploads for metadata\n\nThe MLE kernel itself only takes about 46ms on GPU, but the wall-clock time is 173ms, suggesting 127ms of overhead elsewhere. Looking at the full pipeline, each round involves `sumcheck_polys_batch_eval` with its CPU categorization phase, batched interpolation, TraceCtx setup, and multiple batch evaluations, followed by the fold operations. The bottleneck seems to be spread across these coordination and setup costs rather than the kernels themselves.\n\nComparing the two test cases, the per-round overhead scales with the number of AIRs: going from 99 to 623 AIRs adds about 3ms per round. This comes from the CPU loops that categorize traces and build TraceCtx objects, plus the allocations needed for output matrices in the fold operations.\n\nBreaking down the costs: the `to_device()` calls and matrix allocations account for roughly 1.2ms, the Phase 1 loop adds another 0.5ms, and descriptor uploads contribute 0.3ms—totaling around 2ms per round. But the actual delta between the two cases is 59ms over 20 rounds, leaving about 19ms unaccounted for. The missing piece is likely that `fold_mle_evals` allocates output matrices for every trace still needing folding, and in early rounds at APC 300, that could mean all 623 traces require processing simultaneously.\n\nThe real bottleneck is the sheer volume of small CUDA operations per round: hundreds of `cudaMallocAsync` calls for the folded matrices, pointer uploads to the device, multiple kernel launches, and result copies back. The key issue is that `fold_mle_evals` creates entirely new `DeviceMatrix` objects each round, forcing fresh GPU memory allocations for the half-sized folded results rather than reusing existing buffers.\n\nWhat if we could fold in-place instead? Since the folded output is half the height of the input, we could write the result directly into the first half of the input buffer—reading from row `i` and row `i + H/2` and writing back to row `i`. For column-major storage, this translates to reading from offsets `col*H + i` and `col*H + i + H/2` and writing to `col*H + i`, which is safe because we're always reading from the second half before overwriting the first half.\n\nThe tricky part is that the output needs to be properly contiguous in memory for the next round—the `DeviceMatrix` would need its height updated to `H/2` while keeping the same underlying buffer, with the second half becoming unused data. height and width metadata. If we could just update the height (and keep the same buffer), the folded data would be in positions 0..H/2 per column (in the column-major layout), which is exactly where in-place folding would put it.\n\nBut there's a subtlety: in column-major layout with stride = H, after folding the stride should be H/2. So the data layout would be:\n- Before fold: columns at offsets 0, H, 2H, ... with height H\n- After in-place fold: data at offsets 0, H, 2H, ... but only first H/2 elements of each column are valid\n- For next round, we need: columns at offsets 0, H/2, H, 3H/2, ... with height H/2\n\nThe stride changes from H to H/2! So in-place folding with the same buffer would require the next round to use stride H (not H/2), which means the column data isn't contiguous.\n\nI'm considering two main approaches: either add stride support to DeviceMatrix so we can track non-contiguous layouts and fold in-place without reallocating, or pre-allocate alternating buffers at startup to avoid per-round allocations. The stride approach seems cleaner since it's already common in GPU programming, though it would mean updating all the kernels to handle the stride parameter. the folded matrices (sumcheck_polys_batch_eval, the zerocheck/logup evaluation kernels, etc.) to handle strided matrices. That's a significant change.\n\nLet me think about approach 2 more carefully. Pre-allocate all the per-trace buffers needed across all MLE rounds upfront, then reuse them:\n\nFor trace i with initial height H_i and width W_i:\n- Round 1 needs height H_i/2 → allocate buffer of size (H_i/2) * W_i\n- Round 2 needs height H_i/4 → could reuse the original H_i buffer\n- Round 3 needs height H_i/8 → could reuse the H_i/2 buffer\n- ...\n\nThis is essentially a ping-pong approach with two buffers per trace, alternating between them. The key insight is that across all rounds, each trace uses less than 2x its initial memory since the geometric series sums to less than 2. But since the input traces are already allocated, the overhead is at most 1x the initial size—which might still be too much for GPU memory given 623 traces.\n\nLet me explore batching the output matrix allocation instead. Looking at the `batch_fold` function more carefully, I see it's computing the output height for each matrix and tracking the maximum number of output cells needed, then mapping over the input matrices to extract their dimensions and prepare the output structure. I'm realizing the actual bottleneck here — `mat_evals_per_trace` is a nested vector structure where each trace contains multiple matrices (preprocessed, cached mains, common main), so at APC 300 with ~623 traces and 2-3 matrices per trace, we're looking at roughly 1500 total matrices. Each folding round allocates and frees all of them, which translates to ~3000 CUDA API calls per round at about 1 microsecond each — that's 3 milliseconds per round, and over 20 rounds we're spending 60 milliseconds just on allocation overhead. This is becoming a real performance concern.\n\nAdding in the `sels_per_trace` matrices pushes the per-round overhead to around 4 milliseconds, which compounds to roughly 80 milliseconds over the full MLE loop. That's the missing 59 milliseconds between APC 0 and APC 300. The fix is to pre-allocate a single large buffer before the loop, create output matrices as views into that buffer for each fold operation, and reuse it across rounds — this eliminates thousands of redundant allocation calls.\n\nBut there's a design problem: `DeviceMatrix` owns its buffer, so creating views would require either a new view type or reworking the buffer ownership model. A simpler alternative is to pre-allocate all output matrices upfront based on the initial heights, though that's still many allocations just moved earlier. The real optimization is consolidating to one allocation per fold group per round instead of one per matrix. I'm thinking the cleanest approach is packing all outputs into a single contiguous buffer per fold call...\n\nAllocating bulk buffer and partitioning it...","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1677,"cache_read_input_tokens":106240,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1677},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a8941d98-8943-40ba-ba2b-e8ee56c821e0","uuid":"2ec631b8-a7eb-41ac-a868-d502e75121c7"}
