trace,\n                        &d_inv_lagrange_denoms_r0,\n                        need_rot,\n                    )?;\n                    results.push(folded);\n                }\n\n                // Common main\n                let trace = &air_ctx.common_main;\n                let folded = fold_ple_evals_rotate(\n                    l_skip,\n                    &self.d_omega_skip_pows,\n                    trace,\n                    &d_inv_lagrange_denoms_r0,\n                    need_rot,\n                )?;\n                mem_limit = mem_limit.saturating_sub(folded.buffer().len() * size_of::<EF>());\n                results.push(folded);\n\n                Ok(results)\n            })\n            .collect::<Result<Vec<_>, FoldPleError>>()?;\n        if self.save_memory {\n            self.memory_limit_bytes = mem_limit;\n        }\n\n        // GPU folding for sels_per_trace (rotate=false, only need offset=0)\n        self.sels_per_trace = std::mem::take(&mut self.sels_per_trace_base)\n            .into_iter()\n            .enumerate()\n            .map(|(trace_idx, selectors_cube)| {\n                let n = self.n_per_trace[trace_idx];\n                let num_x = selectors_cube.height();\n                debug_assert_eq!(num_x, 1 << n.max(0));\n                debug_assert_eq!(selectors_cube.width(), 3);\n                let (l, r) = if n.is_negative() {\n                    (\n                        l_skip.wrapping_add_signed(n),\n                        r_0.exp_power_of_2(-n as usize),\n                    )\n                } else {\n                    (l_skip, r_0)\n                };\n                let omega = F::two_adic_generator(l);\n                let is_first = eval_eq_uni_at_one(l, r);\n                let is_last = eval_eq_uni_at_one(l, r * omega);\n                let folded_buf = DeviceBuffer::<EF>::with_capacity(num_x * 3);\n                unsafe {\n                    fold_selectors_round0(\n                        folded_buf.as_mut_ptr(),\n                        selectors_cube.buffer().as_ptr(),\n                        is_first,\n                        is_last,\n                        num_x,\n                    )\n                    .map_err(LogupZerocheckError::FoldSelectorsRound0)?;\n                }\n                Ok(DeviceMatrix::new(Arc::new(folded_buf), num_x, 3))\n            })\n            .collect::<Result<Vec<_>, LogupZerocheckError>>()?;\n\n        // GPU scalar multiplication for eq_xi_per_trace and eq_sharp_per_trace\n        // Compute scalars on CPU (small computation)\n        let eq_r0 = eval_eq_uni(l_skip, self.xi[0], r_0);\n        let eq_sharp_r0 = eval_eq_sharp_uni(&self.omega_skip_pows, &self.xi[..l_skip], r_0);\n        self.eq_ns.push(eq_r0);\n        self.eq_sharp_ns.push(eq_sharp_r0);\n        for tree in self.eq_xis.values_mut() {\n            // trim the back (which corresponds to r_{j-1}) because we don't need it anymore\n            if tree.layers.len() > 1 {\n                tree.layers.pop();\n            }\n        }\n\n        self.mem\n            .emit_metrics_with_label(\"prover.batch_constraints.fold_ple_evals\");\n        Ok(())\n    }\n","numLines":120,"startLine":1410,"totalLines":2197}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01VpcYiBtRqZT3ii8G8maNnq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"OK, so `fold_ple_evals` also does per-trace allocations — one `fold_ple_evals_rotate` per trace per matrix, and one `fold_selectors_round0` per trace. Each of these allocates a DeviceBuffer.\n\nFor APC 300 with 623 traces and ~2-3 matrices per trace: ~1500-1869 allocations in fold_ple_evals + 623 for sels = ~2500.\n\nThis is on top of the MLE round allocations.\n\nBut `fold_ple_evals` runs ONCE (after Round 0), not per MLE round. \n\nSo it's a fixed cost, not amplified by the round count. Now I'm thinking through the best optimization strategy — I have enough data to work with. The core issue at APC 300 is that STARK excluding trace takes 1322ms when I need under 1064ms for 2x scaling, leaving a 258ms gap. The real bottleneck is the per-AIR allocation overhead during MLE rounds, where the main thread fires off roughly 96K CUDA API calls between malloc and free operations, contributing about 59ms of overhead.\n\nLooking at the breakdown more carefully, most of these allocations come from fold output matrices — around 2000 per round across 20 rounds adds up to 40K allocations. There's also per-trace overhead in the sumcheck evaluation where copying main pointers to device happens 623 times per round, totaling about 12K transfers at roughly 1 microsecond each, which accounts for about 12ms. Combined with CPU metadata overhead, the total MLE per-AIR cost at APC 300 comes to roughly 81ms, and scaling that down to APC 0 with 99 AIRs suggests around 6ms of allocation overhead.\n\nThe 71ms difference between these scenarios explains most of the reverse scaling behavior I'm seeing. If I can consolidate those 40K allocations down to just 40 per round through bulk allocation, I'd save roughly 35ms from cudaMallocAsync and another 18ms from cudaFreeAsync, plus potentially 10ms more from reduced memory fragmentation. That's a total expected savings of 50-65ms, which would bring MLE rounds down from 173ms to around 108-123ms and the overall STARK time closer to 1267ms.\n\nBut even with that optimization, the APC 300 to APC 0 ratio would only hit 0.60, giving me 1.68x speedup—still falling short of the 2x target. I need to think bigger. The fold_ple_evals function itself is launching around 1500 separate kernel calls with individual allocations, and fold_selectors_round0 adds another 623, totaling roughly 2100 kernel launches. If I could batch those operations similarly to how the MLE rounds batching works, that might unlock more significant gains, though I need to consider whether fold_ple_evals runs frequently enough to make batching worthwhile.\n\nNow I'm looking at the H2D memory copies—34,458 of them totaling 1341ms of API time. Most of the overhead comes from the sheer number of tiny copies rather than the large ones, and if roughly half of those 30,000 small copies are happening during the STARK exclusion trace phase, that's potentially 75ms of overhead I could recover by batching the `.to_device()` calls on small vectors like pointer arrays and metadata. This is getting complex though, so I should focus on identifying the single most impactful optimization rather than chasing multiple smaller wins.\n\nActually, I'm reconsidering the whole approach—instead of just patching individual allocations, what if I implement a comprehensive arena allocator for the entire MLE round? I'd pre-compute all the buffer sizes needed across every MLE round before the loop starts, then allocate everything upfront in a few large chunks and hand out slices from that arena. This eliminates all the per-round cudaMallocAsync and cudaFreeAsync calls plus the associated pool management overhead, which should save roughly 75-80ms across fold output allocations, temporary device transfers, and descriptor arrays, plus another 10-20ms from reducing memory fragmentation.\n\nThat would bring MLE rounds down from 173ms to around 85-100ms, pushing the overall STARK time to roughly 1235-1255ms and getting us closer to a 1.7x speedup. But even with that gain, I'm still falling short of the 2x target—I'd need to save 258ms total, and no single optimization in MLE rounds alone can bridge that gap. I need to look at this differently and examine whether there's a more fundamental bottleneck I'm missing in the overall breakdown.\n\nLooking at the Openings component more closely, I notice it's at 176ms now versus 413ms at baseline, which is a solid 2.35x improvement. But the scaling from APC 0 to APC 300 is only 1.68x, which suggests it's not scaling as well as it should. Digging into the subcomponents, WHIR scales nicely at 2.19x, but Stacked Reduction is basically flat at 75ms across both APC levels—it was 311ms at baseline and got optimized down by the batch MLE round kernel, but now it's hitting a wall.\n\nThe issue is that Stacked Reduction's cost appears to be dominated by fixed overhead rather than per-AIR work. The CPU NTT reconstruction alone accounts for about 36ms of the 75ms total, and that's constant regardless of AIR count. So even though we went from 99 AIRs to 623 AIRs, the time barely budged. Without rethinking the NTT approach itself, there's not much room to optimize there. I need to step back and look for a different combination of optimizations rather than chasing a single big win.\n\nLooking at what could realistically add up: batching the MLE fold arena allocation could save 70-90ms, batching the fold_ple_evals could save another 10-15ms, and investigating why Round 0 doesn't scale might unlock 20-30ms more. That gets us to around 100-135ms total, which still falls short of the target. But what if instead of just batching the allocation, we eliminate the fold kernel entirely? The kernel works by linearly combining pairs of rows to halve the matrix height. If we could fold in-place during evaluation, we'd avoid allocating output buffers, skip the kernel call, and reduce memory traffic. The challenge is that this requires rethinking how the evaluation happens.\n\nThe simpler approach is an in-place fold where the kernel reads from row i and row H/2+i, then writes back to row i. For column-major layout, that's reading from col*H + i and col*H + H/2 + i, writing to col*H + i. After folding, only the first H/2 rows are valid, but the column stride stays at H instead of shrinking to H/2. This stride mismatch breaks the next round, which expects stride H/2. The fold kernel would need to compact the data afterward to fix the stride.\n\nBut now I'm checking if we can safely compact during folding by writing to col*H/2 + i instead. For column 0, that means writing to positions 0 through H/2 while reading from 0 through H — we'd overwrite data we still need to read. Column 1 is safe since it writes to H/2 through H while reading from H through 2H, with no overlap. So the compaction strategy only works for columns beyond the first one.\n\nThe simplest fix is to allocate one large output buffer upfront and compact everything into it at once, rather than trying to do it in-place. Let me look back at the fold evaluation code to see how this fits in. as a sub-region of a larger buffer, but I need to handle the Arc reference counting carefully to avoid double-frees when sub-matrices drop. The approach would be to allocate one contiguous buffer upfront, compute offsets for each output matrix, then create DeviceMatrix instances that reference sub-regions of that shared buffer through an Arc so the memory stays alive until all references are released.\n\nActually, the assertion in `DeviceMatrix::new` that checks buffer length equals height times width would block this approach. Instead of fighting that constraint, I could sidestep DeviceMatrix entirely for the fold outputs and use a simpler struct that holds the shared buffer plus metadata about where each matrix lives within it—just tracking offsets, heights, and widths for each result. Then update all the consumers to work with this metadata directly rather than expecting DeviceMatrix objects.\n\nThat's cleaner in principle, but it means touching every place that reads from `mat_evals_per_trace`, which could be scattered throughout the codebase. Rather than getting bogged down in those implementation details right now, I should step back and think about this at a higher level—what's the actual optimization goal and which approach gets me closest to the 258ms improvement I need at APC 300 to hit 2x scaling?\n\nLooking at the candidates: bulk fold allocation with per-trace overhead reduction could buy 70-90ms, further GKR input eval work might squeeze out 10-20ms with diminishing returns, batching fold_ple_evals could help by another 10-15ms, and trace commit optimizations have limited upside. None of these alone closes the gap, so I need to pick the most impactful one and plan it properly.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the CUDA API call overhead more systematically. The total across all phases is around 3867ms when I add up cudaMallocAsync, cudaFreeAsync, cudaLaunchKernel, cudaMemcpy, and cudaStreamSync, though most of that is genuinely productive work like synchronization and large transfers. The real overhead from many small API calls is harder to pin down.\n\nBut there's another angle I haven't fully explored: what if I reduce the number of MLE sumcheck rounds themselves? The round count is determined by the maximum trace height minus l_skip, and at APC 300 the traces have varying heights, so there might be room to optimize there.\n\nLooking at how the code currently handles this, it seems like there's already logic to skip rounds for AIRs that have been fully folded down to height 1, checking the partition point where matrices still have height greater than 1. The sumcheck evaluation also has conditional logic based on whether we're past the lifting phase...\n\nExamining the folding logic more closely...\n\nSo once a trace reaches 