{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0141genji4Gh9xSNRfr3CmXA","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Plan: Bulk-Allocate MLE Fold Output Buffers\n2\t\n3\t## Goal\n4\t\n5\tEliminate ~40,000 per-round `cudaMallocAsync`/`cudaFreeAsync` calls in MLE sumcheck fold rounds by replacing per-matrix individual allocations with a single bulk allocation per fold group per round. This targets the main-thread allocation overhead visible in nsight (47K mallocs for 38ms + 49K frees for 21ms = 59ms total), which is the primary cause of MLE Rounds reverse-scaling at APC 300 (173ms vs 114ms at APC 0 despite 2x less total work).\n6\t\n7\t**Expected saving**: 50–65ms at APC 300 (MLE Rounds 173ms → ~108–123ms).\n8\t\n9\t## Current Code Path\n10\t\n11\t### MLE round loop (`mod.rs:631–648`)\n12\t\n13\tEach round calls:\n14\t1. `sumcheck_polys_batch_eval(round, r[round-1])` — evaluates sumcheck polynomials for all traces\n15\t2. `compute_batch_s_poly(...)` — CPU batching\n16\t3. `fold_mle_evals(round, r_round)` — halves all trace matrix heights via GPU kernel\n17\t\n18\t### `fold_mle_evals` (`mod.rs:2000–2091`)\n19\t\n20\tThe inner `batch_fold` closure (line 2002–2048):\n21\t1. **Counts foldable matrices**: `partition_point(|mat| mat.height() > 1)` → `num_matrices`\n22\t2. **Allocates per-matrix output buffers** (line 2014):\n23\t   ```rust\n24\t   let output_mat = DeviceMatrix::<EF>::with_capacity(output_height, width);\n25\t   ```\n26\t   At APC 300: ~1246 matrices for mat_evals + ~623 for sels = **~1869 individual allocations per round**.\n27\t3. **Collects pointer arrays**, uploads 4 small H2D buffers (input_ptrs, output_ptrs, heights, widths)\n28\t4. **Single `batch_fold_mle` kernel launch** — one kernel folds all matrices\n29\t5. **Appends unfoldable matrices** (height=1) unchanged\n30\t\n31\tOver ~20 MLE rounds: **~37,380 allocs + ~37,380 frees = ~74,760 CUDA memory API calls**.\n32\t\n33\t### Consumers of `mat_evals_per_trace`\n34\t\n35\t`mat_evals_per_trace: Vec<Vec<DeviceMatrix<EF>>>` is accessed at:\n36\t- `sumcheck_polys_batch_eval` (line 1574): `.iter()` for metadata loop — reads `buffer().as_ptr()`, `height()`, `width()` per matrix\n37\t- `sumcheck_polys_batch_eval` (line 1730): `&self.mat_evals_per_trace[meta.trace_idx]` — indexed access for Case B\n38\t- `fold_mle_evals` (line 2051–2066): `std::mem::take`, flatten, fold, restructure\n39\t- `into_column_openings` (line 2107): `std::mem::take`, D2H copy via `transport_matrix_d2h_col_major(&mat)`\n40\t- Memory accounting (line 2069): `.buffer().len()` sum\n41\t\n42\t`sels_per_trace: Vec<DeviceMatrix<EF>>` has the same access patterns in `batch_fold` and `sumcheck_polys_batch_eval`.\n43\t\n44\t### Why it's slow at APC 300\n45\t\n46\tAt APC 300 (623 AIR instances, ~1869 matrices), each fold round allocates ~1869 output buffers via `cudaMallocAsync`. Even though the CUDA pool allocator caches memory (making per-call overhead ~0.8μs), the aggregate over 20 rounds is ~30K alloc calls + ~30K free calls = ~50ms. Combined with CPU-side metadata iteration over 623 traces per round, the total MLE per-round overhead is ~5.5ms at APC 300 vs ~1.5ms at APC 0 (99 AIRs, ~200 matrices per fold).\n47\t\n48\t## Changes\n49\t\n50\t### Change 1: Add `ArenaMatrix<T>` type (`crates/cuda-backend/src/base.rs`)\n51\t\n52\tA lightweight non-owning matrix type that stores a raw pointer + dimensions:\n53\t\n54\t```rust\n55\tpub struct ArenaMatrix<T> {\n56\t    ptr: *mut T,\n57\t    height: usize,\n58\t    width: usize,\n59\t}\n60\t```\n61\t\n62\tMethods: `as_ptr() -> *const T`, `as_mut_ptr() -> *mut T`, `height()`, `width()`, `buffer_len() -> usize` (returns `height * width`). Implement `Send + Sync` (same safety contract as DeviceMatrix). Does NOT implement Drop (no deallocation — the arena owns the memory).\n63\t\n64\tAdd a `to_host(&self) -> Result<Vec<T>, MemCopyError>` method that performs a D2H copy of `height * width` elements from `ptr`.\n65\t\n66\t**Why**: Decouples matrix metadata from buffer ownership. The arena holds the big buffer; ArenaMatrices are cheap views into it.\n67\t\n68\t### Change 2: Add `FoldArena<T>` type (`crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs`)\n69\t\n70\t```rust\n71\tstruct FoldArena<T> {\n72\t    /// The big buffer(s) backing all ArenaMatrix views.\n73\t    /// The most recent is at the end; older ones are kept alive until\n74\t    /// all ArenaMatrix references are consumed (end of MLE rounds).\n75\t    buffers: Vec<DeviceBuffer<T>>,\n76\t}\n77\t```\n78\t\n79\tMethods:\n80\t- `allocate_bulk(total_cells: usize) -> *mut T`: Allocates one `DeviceBuffer` of `total_cells`, pushes to `self.buffers`, returns `as_mut_ptr()`.\n81\t- When `FoldArena` drops, all buffers are freed via their normal `DeviceBuffer::drop`.\n82\t\n83\t**Why**: Centralizes lifetime management. All arena buffers stay alive until the prover struct is dropped (after `into_column_openings` consumes the data).\n84\t\n85\t### Change 3: Change `mat_evals_per_trace` and `sels_per_trace` types\n86\t\n87\tIn `LogupZerocheckGpu`:\n88\t```rust\n89\t// Before:\n90\tmat_evals_per_trace: Vec<Vec<DeviceMatrix<EF>>>,\n91\tsels_per_trace: Vec<DeviceMatrix<EF>>,\n92\t\n93\t// After:\n94\tmat_evals_per_trace: Vec<Vec<ArenaMatrix<EF>>>,\n95\tsels_per_trace: Vec<ArenaMatrix<EF>>,\n96\tfold_arena: FoldArena<EF>,\n97\t```\n98\t\n99\t**Why**: `ArenaMatrix` is non-owning, so dropping `mat_evals_per_trace` does NOT free GPU memory. The `fold_arena` field owns all the GPU memory and frees it when the prover is dropped.\n100\t\n101\t### Change 4: Update `fold_ple_evals` (`mod.rs:1412–1528`)\n102\t\n103\tThe initial population of `mat_evals_per_trace` calls `fold_ple_evals_rotate` per trace, which returns `DeviceMatrix<EF>`. Change to:\n104\t\n105\t1. Collect all folded output sizes (per trace, per matrix)\n106\t2. Allocate one big buffer via `fold_arena.allocate_bulk(total_cells)`\n107\t3. For each per-trace `fold_ple_evals_rotate`, pass the destination pointer from the arena instead of letting it allocate internally\n108\t\n109\tThis requires checking how `fold_ple_evals_rotate` works. If it allocates internally and returns a `DeviceMatrix`, we have two sub-options:\n110\t\n111\t**Option A (simpler)**: Let `fold_ple_evals_rotate` allocate as today, then copy the data into the arena and drop the temporary `DeviceMatrix`. This adds a D2D copy but only runs once.\n112\t\n113\t**Option B (better)**: Modify `fold_ple_evals_rotate` to accept a pre-allocated output pointer. This avoids the extra allocation entirely.\n114\t\n115\tSimilarly for `fold_selectors_round0` (line 1497–1508), which allocates `DeviceBuffer::with_capacity` and creates `DeviceMatrix::new`.\n116\t\n117\tFor `sels_per_trace` initialization, allocate one arena buffer for all 623 selector matrices (each 3 × num_x elements) and pass sub-pointers.\n118\t\n119\t**Why**: Eliminates the initial ~2500 allocations from `fold_ple_evals` as well.\n120\t\n121\t### Change 5: Rewrite `batch_fold` closure in `fold_mle_evals` (`mod.rs:2002–2048`)\n122\t\n123\tReplace per-matrix allocation with arena-based bulk allocation:\n124\t\n125\t```rust\n126\tlet batch_fold = |input_mats: Vec<ArenaMatrix<EF>>,\n127\t                  arena: &mut FoldArena<EF>|\n128\t    -> Result<Vec<ArenaMatrix<EF>>, LogupZerocheckError>\n129\t{\n130\t    let num_matrices = input_mats.partition_point(|mat| mat.height() > 1);\n131\t\n132\t    // Compute total output cells and per-matrix metadata\n133\t    let mut total_cells = 0usize;\n134\t    let mut offsets = Vec::with_capacity(num_matrices);\n135\t    let mut log_output_heights = Vec::with_capacity(num_matrices);\n136\t    let mut widths = Vec::with_capacity(num_matrices);\n137\t    let mut max_output_cells = 0usize;\n138\t\n139\t    for mat in input_mats.iter().take(num_matrices) {\n140\t        let h = mat.height() >> 1;\n141\t        let w = mat.width();\n142\t        let cells = h * w;\n143\t        offsets.push(total_cells);\n144\t        total_cells += cells;\n145\t        max_output_cells = max(max_output_cells, cells);\n146\t        log_output_heights.push(h.ilog2() as u8);\n147\t        widths.push(w as u32);\n148\t    }\n149\t\n150\t    // ONE allocation instead of ~1869\n151\t    let arena_ptr = arena.allocate_bulk(total_cells);\n152\t\n153\t    // Build output matrices as arena views\n154\t    let mut output_mats: Vec<ArenaMatrix<EF>> = offsets.iter().enumerate().map(|(i, &off)| {\n155\t        let h = (input_mats[i].height() >> 1);\n156\t        let w = input_mats[i].width();\n157\t        ArenaMatrix::new(unsafe { arena_ptr.add(off) }, h, w)\n158\t    }).collect();\n159\t\n160\t    // Collect pointers for CUDA kernel\n161\t    let input_ptrs: Vec<_> = input_mats.iter().take(num_matrices)\n162\t        .map(|mat| mat.as_ptr()).collect();\n163\t    let output_ptrs: Vec<_> = output_mats.iter()\n164\t        .map(|mat| mat.as_mut_ptr()).collect();\n165\t\n166\t    // Upload and launch (same as before)\n167\t    let d_input_ptrs = input_ptrs.to_device()?;\n168\t    let d_output_ptrs = output_ptrs.to_device()?;\n169\t    let d_log_output_heights = log_output_heights.to_device()?;\n170\t    let d_widths = widths.to_device()?;\n171\t\n172\t    unsafe {\n173\t        batch_fold_mle(\n174\t            &d_input_ptrs, &d_output_ptrs, &d_widths,\n175\t            num_matrices.try_into().unwrap(),\n176\t            &d_log_output_heights,\n177\t            max_output_cells.try_into().unwrap(),\n178\t            r_round,\n179\t        ).map_err(LogupZerocheckError::BatchFoldMle)?;\n180\t    }\n181\t\n182\t    // Append unfoldable matrices (height=1) unchanged\n183\t    output_mats.extend_from_slice(&input_mats[num_matrices..]);\n184\t    Ok(output_mats)\n185\t};\n186\t```\n187\t\n188\t**Key difference**: ONE `cudaMallocAsync` per fold call instead of ~1869. Over 20 rounds × 2 groups (mat_evals + sels) = **40 allocations instead of ~37,380**.\n189\t\n190\t### Change 6: Update `sumcheck_polys_batch_eval` consumers (`mod.rs:1536–1922`)\n191\t\n192\tMechanical replacements:\n193\t- `mats[i].buffer().as_ptr()` → `mats[i].as_ptr()`\n194\t- `mats[i].height()` → `mats[i].height()` (unchanged)\n195\t- `mats[i].width()` → `mats[i].width()` (unchanged)\n196\t- `m.buffer().as_ptr().wrapping_add(col * m.height())` → `m.as_ptr().wrapping_add(col * m.height())`\n197\t\n198\tThese are ~15 call sites that access buffer pointers.\n199\t\n200\t### Change 7: Update `into_column_openings` (`mod.rs:2098–2172`)\n201\t\n202\tReplace `transport_matrix_d2h_col_major(&mat)` with a direct D2H copy from the `ArenaMatrix` pointer:\n203\t\n204\t```rust\n205\tlet host_data = mat.to_host()?; // Uses ArenaMatrix::to_host\n206\tlet mat_host = ColMajorMatrix::new(host_data, mat.width());\n207\t```\n208\t\n209\tAt this point all matrices have height=1, so these are tiny copies (width elements each).\n210\t\n211\t### Change 8: Update `save_memory` accounting (`mod.rs:2067–2075`)\n212\t\n213\tReplace `m.buffer().len() * size_of::<EF>()` with `m.buffer_len() * size_of::<EF>()`.\n214\t\n215\t## Invariants\n216\t\n217\t1. **No double-free**: `ArenaMatrix` does NOT free memory on drop. Only `FoldArena::drop` frees the big buffers. This eliminates the double-free risk that would occur with sub-DeviceBuffer approaches.\n218\t\n219\t2. **Arena lifetime**: `FoldArena` is a field of `LogupZerocheckGpu` and outlives all `ArenaMatrix` references (stored in `mat_evals_per_trace` and `sels_per_trace` on the same struct). `into_column_openings` consumes `self`, so the arena data is read before the struct is dropped.\n220\t\n221\t3. **Correctness**: The CUDA `batch_fold_mle` kernel receives the same pointer arrays as before. The kernel doesn't care whether output pointers come from individual allocations or a single bulk allocation. The fold algorithm is unchanged.\n222\t\n223\t4. **Unfoldable matrices**: Matrices that reach height=1 are carried forward by value (their `ArenaMatrix` data points into an earlier arena buffer that remains alive). The `partition_point` logic is preserved.\n224\t\n225\t5. **Memory accounting**: The `save_memory` path uses `buffer_len()` which returns `height * width` (the logical size), matching the current `buffer().len()` semantics.\n226\t\n227\t6. **APC 0 behavior**: At APC 0 (~99 AIRs, ~200 matrices), the bulk allocation still applies but the savings are minimal (~3ms). No regression expected since the big buffer allocation amortizes the overhead of 200 individual allocations.\n228\t\n229\t## Measurement Plan\n230\t\n231\t**Benchmark commands** (in powdr repo):\n232\t```bash\n233\tRUST_LOG=info APC=300 openvm-riscv/scripts/run_pairing.sh  # Target metric\n234\tRUST_LOG=info APC=0 openvm-riscv/scripts/run_pairing.sh    # Regression check\n235\t```\n236\t\n237\t**Analysis**:\n238\t```bash\n239\tpython3 /home/georg/spec.py results/pairing/apc300/metrics.json after_apc300\n240\tpython3 /home/georg/spec.py results/pairing/apc000/metrics.json after_apc000\n241\t```\n242\t\n243\t**Expected results**:\n244\t- MLE Rounds at APC 300: 173ms → 108–123ms (30–40% reduction)\n245\t- STARK excl trace at APC 300: 1322ms → ~1257–1272ms\n246\t- APC 0: No significant change (±5ms noise)\n247\t\n248\t**Nsight verification**:\n249\t- Main-thread `cudaMallocAsync` call count should drop from ~47K to ~7K (eliminating ~40K fold allocs)\n250\t- Main-thread `cudaFreeAsync` call count should drop similarly\n251\t\n252\t## Rollback Criteria\n253\t\n254\tRevert if:\n255\t- MLE Rounds improvement at APC 300 is < 20ms (below noise threshold for a 173ms metric)\n256\t- STARK excl trace improvement at APC 300 is < 30ms\n257\t- APC 0 regresses by > 15ms\n258\t- Correctness failures (proof verification fails at any APC config)\n259\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a8941d98-8943-40ba-ba2b-e8ee56c821e0","uuid":"3ff8615e-0599-4417-9377-706a74c54dbc","timestamp":"2026-04-14T10:19:02.074Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt-results/2026-04-14-1200-bulk-alloc-mle-fold-buffers/plan.md","content":"# Plan: Bulk-Allocate MLE Fold Output Buffers\n\n## Goal\n\nEliminate ~40,000 per-round `cudaMallocAsync`/`cudaFreeAsync` calls in MLE sumcheck fold rounds by replacing per-matrix individual allocations with a single bulk allocation per fold group per round. This targets the main-thread allocation overhead visible in nsight (47K mallocs for 38ms + 49K frees for 21ms = 59ms total), which is the primary cause of MLE Rounds reverse-scaling at APC 300 (173ms vs 114ms at APC 0 despite 2x less total work).\n\n**Expected saving**: 50–65ms at APC 300 (MLE Rounds 173ms → ~108–123ms).\n\n## Current Code Path\n\n### MLE round loop (`mod.rs:631–648`)\n\nEach round calls:\n1. `sumcheck_polys_batch_eval(round, r[round-1])` — evaluates sumcheck polynomials for all traces\n2. `compute_batch_s_poly(...)` — CPU batching\n3. `fold_mle_evals(round, r_round)` — halves all trace matrix heights via GPU kernel\n\n### `fold_mle_evals` (`mod.rs:2000–2091`)\n\nThe inner `batch_fold` closure (line 2002–2048):\n1. **Counts foldable matrices**: `partition_point(|mat| mat.height() > 1)` → `num_matrices`\n2. **Allocates per-matrix output buffers** (line 2014):\n   ```rust\n   let output_mat = DeviceMatrix::<EF>::with_capacity(output_height, width);\n   ```\n   At APC 300: ~1246 matrices for mat_evals + ~623 for sels = **~1869 individual allocations per round**.\n3. **Collects pointer arrays**, uploads 4 small H2D buffers (input_ptrs, output_ptrs, heights, widths)\n4. **Single `batch_fold_mle` kernel launch** — one kernel folds all matrices\n5. **Appends unfoldable matrices** (height=1) unchanged\n\nOver ~20 MLE rounds: **~37,380 allocs + ~37,380 frees = ~74,760 CUDA memory API calls**.\n\n### Consumers of `mat_evals_per_trace`\n\n`mat_evals_per_trace: Vec<Vec<DeviceMatrix<EF>>>` is accessed at:\n- `sumcheck_polys_batch_eval` (line 1574): `.iter()` for metadata loop — reads `buffer().as_ptr()`, `height()`, `width()` per matrix\n- `sumcheck_polys_batch_eval` (line 1730): `&self.mat_evals_per_trace[meta.trace_idx]` — indexed access for Case B\n- `fold_mle_evals` (line 2051–2066): `std::mem::take`, flatten, fold, restructure\n- `into_column_openings` (line 2107): `std::mem::take`, D2H copy via `transport_matrix_d2h_col_major(&mat)`\n- Memory accounting (line 2069): `.buffer().len()` sum\n\n`sels_per_trace: Vec<DeviceMatrix<EF>>` has the same access patterns in `batch_fold` and `sumcheck_polys_batch_eval`.\n\n### Why it's slow at APC 300\n\nAt APC 300 (623 AIR instances, ~1869 matrices), each fold round allocates ~1869 output buffers via `cudaMallocAsync`. Even though the CUDA pool allocator caches memory (making per-call overhead ~0.8μs), the aggregate over 20 rounds is ~30K alloc calls + ~30K free calls = ~50ms. Combined with CPU-side metadata iteration over 623 traces per round, the total MLE per-round overhead is ~5.5ms at APC 300 vs ~1.5ms at APC 0 (99 AIRs, ~200 matrices per fold).\n\n## Changes\n\n### Change 1: Add `ArenaMatrix<T>` type (`crates/cuda-backend/src/base.rs`)\n\nA lightweight non-owning matrix type that stores a raw pointer + dimensions:\n\n```rust\npub struct ArenaMatrix<T> {\n    ptr: *mut T,\n    height: usize,\n    width: usize,\n}\n```\n\nMethods: `as_ptr() -> *const T`, `as_mut_ptr() -> *mut T`, `height()`, `width()`, `buffer_len() -> usize` (returns `height * width`). Implement `Send + Sync` (same safety contract as DeviceMatrix). Does NOT implement Drop (no deallocation — the arena owns the memory).\n\nAdd a `to_host(&self) -> Result<Vec<T>, MemCopyError>` method that performs a D2H copy of `height * width` elements from `ptr`.\n\n**Why**: Decouples matrix metadata from buffer ownership. The arena holds the big buffer; ArenaMatrices are cheap views into it.\n\n### Change 2: Add `FoldArena<T>` type (`crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs`)\n\n```rust\nstruct FoldArena<T> {\n    /// The big buffer(s) backing all ArenaMatrix views.\n    /// The most recent is at the end; older ones are kept alive until\n    /// all ArenaMatrix references are consumed (end of MLE rounds).\n    buffers: Vec<DeviceBuffer<T>>,\n}\n```\n\nMethods:\n- `allocate_bulk(total_cells: usize) -> *mut T`: Allocates one `DeviceBuffer` of `total_cells`, pushes to `self.buffers`, returns `as_mut_ptr()`.\n- When `FoldArena` drops, all buffers are freed via their normal `DeviceBuffer::drop`.\n\n**Why**: Centralizes lifetime management. All arena buffers stay alive until the prover struct is dropped (after `into_column_openings` consumes the data).\n\n### Change 3: Change `mat_evals_per_trace` and `sels_per_trace` types\n\nIn `LogupZerocheckGpu`:\n```rust\n// Before:\nmat_evals_per_trace: Vec<Vec<DeviceMatrix<EF>>>,\nsels_per_trace: Vec<DeviceMatrix<EF>>,\n\n// After:\nmat_evals_per_trace: Vec<Vec<ArenaMatrix<EF>>>,\nsels_per_trace: Vec<ArenaMatrix<EF>>,\nfold_arena: FoldArena<EF>,\n```\n\n**Why**: `ArenaMatrix` is non-owning, so dropping `mat_evals_per_trace` does NOT free GPU memory. The `fold_arena` field owns all the GPU memory and frees it when the prover is dropped.\n\n### Change 4: Update `fold_ple_evals` (`mod.rs:1412–1528`)\n\nThe initial population of `mat_evals_per_trace` calls `fold_ple_evals_rotate` per trace, which returns `DeviceMatrix<EF>`. Change to:\n\n1. Collect all folded output sizes (per trace, per matrix)\n2. Allocate one big buffer via `fold_arena.allocate_bulk(total_cells)`\n3. For each per-trace `fold_ple_evals_rotate`, pass the destination pointer from the arena instead of letting it allocate internally\n\nThis requires checking how `fold_ple_evals_rotate` works. If it allocates internally and returns a `DeviceMatrix`, we have two sub-options:\n\n**Option A (simpler)**: Let `fold_ple_evals_rotate` allocate as today, then copy the data into the arena and drop the temporary `DeviceMatrix`. This adds a D2D copy but only runs once.\n\n**Option B (better)**: Modify `fold_ple_evals_rotate` to accept a pre-allocated output pointer. This avoids the extra allocation entirely.\n\nSimilarly for `fold_selectors_round0` (line 1497–1508), which allocates `DeviceBuffer::with_capacity` and creates `DeviceMatrix::new`.\n\nFor `sels_per_trace` initialization, allocate one arena buffer for all 623 selector matrices (each 3 × num_x elements) and pass sub-pointers.\n\n**Why**: Eliminates the initial ~2500 allocations from `fold_ple_evals` as well.\n\n### Change 5: Rewrite `batch_fold` closure in `fold_mle_evals` (`mod.rs:2002–2048`)\n\nReplace per-matrix allocation with arena-based bulk allocation:\n\n```rust\nlet batch_fold = |input_mats: Vec<ArenaMatrix<EF>>,\n                  arena: &mut FoldArena<EF>|\n    -> Result<Vec<ArenaMatrix<EF>>, LogupZerocheckError>\n{\n    let num_matrices = input_mats.partition_point(|mat| mat.height() > 1);\n\n    // Compute total output cells and per-matrix metadata\n    let mut total_cells = 0usize;\n    let mut offsets = Vec::with_capacity(num_matrices);\n    let mut log_output_heights = Vec::with_capacity(num_matrices);\n    let mut widths = Vec::with_capacity(num_matrices);\n    let mut max_output_cells = 0usize;\n\n    for mat in input_mats.iter().take(num_matrices) {\n        let h = mat.height() >> 1;\n        let w = mat.width();\n        let cells = h * w;\n        offsets.push(total_cells);\n        total_cells += cells;\n        max_output_cells = max(max_output_cells, cells);\n        log_output_heights.push(h.ilog2() as u8);\n        widths.push(w as u32);\n    }\n\n    // ONE allocation instead of ~1869\n    let arena_ptr = arena.allocate_bulk(total_cells);\n\n    // Build output matrices as arena views\n    let mut output_mats: Vec<ArenaMatrix<EF>> = offsets.iter().enumerate().map(|(i, &off)| {\n        let h = (input_mats[i].height() >> 1);\n        let w = input_mats[i].width();\n        ArenaMatrix::new(unsafe { arena_ptr.add(off) }, h, w)\n    }).collect();\n\n    // Collect pointers for CUDA kernel\n    let input_ptrs: Vec<_> = input_mats.iter().take(num_matrices)\n        .map(|mat| mat.as_ptr()).collect();\n    let output_ptrs: Vec<_> = output_mats.iter()\n        .map(|mat| mat.as_mut_ptr()).collect();\n\n    // Upload and launch (same as before)\n    let d_input_ptrs = input_ptrs.to_device()?;\n    let d_output_ptrs = output_ptrs.to_device()?;\n    let d_log_output_heights = log_output_heights.to_device()?;\n    let d_widths = widths.to_device()?;\n\n    unsafe {\n        batch_fold_mle(\n            &d_input_ptrs, &d_output_ptrs, &d_widths,\n            num_matrices.try_into().unwrap(),\n            &d_log_output_heights,\n            max_output_cells.try_into().unwrap(),\n            r_round,\n        ).map_err(LogupZerocheckError::BatchFoldMle)?;\n    }\n\n    // Append unfoldable matrices (height=1) unchanged\n    output_mats.extend_from_slice(&input_mats[num_matrices..]);\n    Ok(output_mats)\n};\n```\n\n**Key difference**: ONE `cudaMallocAsync` per fold call instead of ~1869. Over 20 rounds × 2 groups (mat_evals + sels) = **40 allocations instead of ~37,380**.\n\n### Change 6: Update `sumcheck_polys_batch_eval` consumers (`mod.rs:1536–1922`)\n\nMechanical replacements:\n- `mats[i].buffer().as_ptr()` → `mats[i].as_ptr()`\n- `mats[i].height()` → `mats[i].height()` (unchanged)\n- `mats[i].width()` → `mats[i].width()` (unchanged)\n- `m.buffer().as_ptr().wrapping_add(col * m.height())` → `m.as_ptr().wrapping_add(col * m.height())`\n\nThese are ~15 call sites that access buffer pointers.\n\n### Change 7: Update `into_column_openings` (`mod.rs:2098–2172`)\n\nReplace `transport_matrix_d2h_col_major(&mat)` with a direct D2H copy from the `ArenaMatrix` pointer:\n\n```rust\nlet host_data = mat.to_host()?; // Uses ArenaMatrix::to_host\nlet mat_host = ColMajorMatrix::new(host_data, mat.width());\n```\n\nAt this point all matrices have height=1, so these are tiny copies (width elements each).\n\n### Change 8: Update `save_memory` accounting (`mod.rs:2067–2075`)\n\nReplace `m.buffer().len() * size_of::<EF>()` with `m.buffer_len() * size_of::<EF>()`.\n\n## Invariants\n\n1. **No double-free**: `ArenaMatrix` does NOT free memory on drop. Only `FoldArena::drop` frees the big buffers. This eliminates the double-free risk that would occur with sub-DeviceBuffer approaches.\n\n2. **Arena lifetime**: `FoldArena` is a field of `LogupZerocheckGpu` and outlives all `ArenaMatrix` references (stored in `mat_evals_per_trace` and `sels_per_trace` on the same struct). `into_column_openings` consumes `self`, so the arena data is read before the struct is dropped.\n\n3. **Correctness**: The CUDA `batch_fold_mle` kernel receives the same pointer arrays as before. The kernel doesn't care whether output pointers come from individual allocations or a single bulk allocation. The fold algorithm is unchanged.\n\n4. **Unfoldable matrices**: Matrices that reach height=1 are carried forward by value (their `ArenaMatrix` data points into an earlier arena buffer that remains alive). The `partition_point` logic is preserved.\n\n5. **Memory accounting**: The `save_memory` path uses `buffer_len()` which returns `height * width` (the logical size), matching the current `buffer().len()` semantics.\n\n6. **APC 0 behavior**: At APC 0 (~99 AIRs, ~200 matrices), the bulk allocation still applies but the savings are minimal (~3ms). No regression expected since the big buffer allocation amortizes the overhead of 200 individual allocations.\n\n## Measurement Plan\n\n**Benchmark commands** (in powdr repo):\n```bash\nRUST_LOG=info APC=300 openvm-riscv/scripts/run_pairing.sh  # Target metric\nRUST_LOG=info APC=0 openvm-riscv/scripts/run_pairing.sh    # Regression check\n```\n\n**Analysis**:\n```bash\npython3 /home/georg/spec.py results/pairing/apc300/metrics.json after_apc300\npython3 /home/georg/spec.py results/pairing/apc000/metrics.json after_apc000\n```\n\n**Expected results**:\n- MLE Rounds at APC 300: 173ms → 108–123ms (30–40% reduction)\n- STARK excl trace at APC 300: 1322ms → ~1257–1272ms\n- APC 0: No significant change (±5ms noise)\n\n**Nsight verification**:\n- Main-thread `cudaMallocAsync` call count should drop from ~47K to ~7K (eliminating ~40K fold allocs)\n- Main-thread `cudaFreeAsync` call count should drop similarly\n\n## Rollback Criteria\n\nRevert if:\n- MLE Rounds improvement at APC 300 is < 20ms (below noise threshold for a 173ms metric)\n- STARK excl trace improvement at APC 300 is < 30ms\n- APC 0 regresses by > 15ms\n- Correctness failures (proof verification fails at any APC config)\n","numLines":259,"startLine":1,"totalLines":259}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01F4eBGApuBUixzdqBDQSoLg","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Now let me update the plan addressing all review findings."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5259,"cache_read_input_tokens":162209,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":5259},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a8941d98-8943-40ba-ba2b-e8ee56c821e0","uuid":"78510746-10ab-47ee-ad42-870e46ac20a4"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01F4eBGApuBUixzdqBDQSoLg","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01GwmeKTLsybVi51qj7t5Pi4","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt-results/2026-04-14-1200-bulk-alloc-mle-fold-buffers/plan.md","content":"# Plan: Bulk-Allocate MLE Fold Output Buffers\n\n## Goal\n\nEliminate ~37,000 per-round `cudaMallocAsync`/`cudaFreeAsync` calls in MLE sumcheck fold rounds by replacing per-matrix individual allocations with a single bulk allocation per fold group per round. This targets the main-thread allocation overhead visible in nsight (47K mallocs for 38ms + 49K frees for 21ms = 59ms total), which is the primary cause of MLE Rounds reverse-scaling at APC 300 (173ms vs 114ms at APC 0 despite 2x less total work).\n\n**Expected saving**: 40–55ms at APC 300 (MLE Rounds 173ms → ~118–133ms).\n\n**Scope**: Only the MLE fold loop (`fold_mle_evals`, rounds 1..n_max). The one-time `fold_ple_evals` (round 0, ~2500 allocs, ~2ms overhead) is NOT changed — it continues to use `DeviceMatrix<EF>` and is handled by a transitional type in the first MLE fold round.\n\n## Current Code Path\n\n### MLE round loop (`mod.rs:631–648`)\n\nEach round calls:\n1. `sumcheck_polys_batch_eval(round, r[round-1])` — evaluates sumcheck polynomials for all traces\n2. `compute_batch_s_poly(...)` — CPU batching\n3. `fold_mle_evals(round, r_round)` — halves all trace matrix heights via GPU kernel\n\n### `fold_mle_evals` (`mod.rs:2000–2091`)\n\nThe inner `batch_fold` closure (line 2002–2048):\n1. **Counts foldable matrices**: `partition_point(|mat| mat.height() > 1)` → `num_matrices`\n2. **Allocates per-matrix output buffers** (line 2014):\n   ```rust\n   let output_mat = DeviceMatrix::<EF>::with_capacity(output_height, width);\n   ```\n   At APC 300: ~1246 matrices for mat_evals + ~623 for sels = **~1869 individual allocations per round**.\n3. **Collects pointer arrays**, uploads 4 small H2D buffers (input_ptrs, output_ptrs, heights, widths)\n4. **Single `batch_fold_mle` kernel launch** — one kernel folds all matrices\n5. **Appends unfoldable matrices** (height=1) unchanged\n\nOver ~20 MLE rounds: **~37,380 allocs + ~37,380 frees = ~74,760 CUDA memory API calls**.\n\n### Consumers of `mat_evals_per_trace`\n\n`mat_evals_per_trace: Vec<Vec<DeviceMatrix<EF>>>` is accessed at:\n- `sumcheck_polys_batch_eval` (line 1574): `.iter()` for metadata loop — reads `buffer().as_ptr()`, `height()`, `width()` per matrix\n- `sumcheck_polys_batch_eval` (line 1730): `&self.mat_evals_per_trace[meta.trace_idx]` — indexed access for Case B\n- `fold_mle_evals` (line 2051–2066): `std::mem::take`, flatten, fold, restructure\n- `into_column_openings` (line 2107): `std::mem::take`, D2H copy via `transport_matrix_d2h_col_major(&mat)`\n- Memory accounting (line 2069): `.buffer().len()` sum\n\n`sels_per_trace: Vec<DeviceMatrix<EF>>` has the same access patterns in `batch_fold` and `sumcheck_polys_batch_eval`.\n\n### Why it's slow at APC 300\n\nAt APC 300 (623 AIR instances, ~1869 matrices), each fold round allocates ~1869 output buffers via `cudaMallocAsync`. Even though the CUDA pool allocator caches memory (making per-call overhead ~0.8μs), the aggregate over 20 rounds is ~30K alloc calls + ~30K free calls = ~50ms. Combined with CPU-side metadata iteration over 623 traces per round, the total MLE per-round overhead is ~5.5ms at APC 300 vs ~1.5ms at APC 0 (99 AIRs, ~200 matrices per fold).\n\nQuantitative estimate from nsight: the fold accounts for ~(37K/47K) × 38ms allocs + ~(37K/49K) × 21ms frees ≈ 31ms + 16ms = **~47ms** of pure CUDA memory API overhead. Additional savings from reduced memory pool fragmentation bring the total to ~40–55ms.\n\n## Changes\n\n### Change 1: Add `ArenaMatrix<T>` type (`crates/cuda-backend/src/base.rs`)\n\nA lightweight non-owning matrix type that stores a raw pointer + dimensions:\n\n```rust\n#[derive(Clone, Copy)]\npub struct ArenaMatrix<T> {\n    ptr: *mut T,\n    height: usize,\n    width: usize,\n}\n\nunsafe impl<T> Send for ArenaMatrix<T> {}\nunsafe impl<T> Sync for ArenaMatrix<T> {}\n```\n\nMethods: `new(ptr, height, width)`, `as_ptr() -> *const T`, `as_mut_ptr() -> *mut T`, `height()`, `width()`, `buffer_len() -> usize` (returns `height * width`).\n\nAdd a `to_host(&self) -> Result<Vec<T>, MemCopyError>` method that performs a D2H copy of `height * width` elements from `ptr`. This requires calling `cudaMemcpyAsync` directly (import from `openvm_cuda_common::stream::{cudaStreamPerThread, cudaStream_t}` and `openvm_cuda_common::copy::cudaMemcpy_d2h` or equivalent), since there is no backing `DeviceBuffer` to delegate to.\n\nDerive `Copy + Clone` — all fields are `Copy`-safe. This is needed for `extend_from_slice` on unfoldable height-1 matrices in `batch_fold`.\n\n**Why `DeviceMatrixView` is not used**: The existing `DeviceMatrixView<'a, T>` in `base.rs:117-156` has a lifetime parameter `'a`. Using it for `mat_evals_per_trace` would require adding a lifetime to `LogupZerocheckGpu`, which would create a self-referential borrow (the arena and the views are both fields on the same struct). `ArenaMatrix` avoids this by omitting the lifetime — safety is upheld structurally because `FoldArena` (the owner) and `mat_evals_per_trace` (the views) are fields on the same struct, with the struct's `into_column_openings` consuming all views before drop.\n\n### Change 2: Add `MatrixRef<T>` enum (`crates/cuda-backend/src/base.rs`)\n\nTo handle the transition from `DeviceMatrix` (output of `fold_ple_evals`) to `ArenaMatrix` (output of MLE fold), use an enum:\n\n```rust\n#[derive(Clone)]\npub enum MatrixRef<T> {\n    Owned(DeviceMatrix<T>),\n    Arena(ArenaMatrix<T>),\n}\n```\n\nMethods (delegating to inner type): `as_ptr()`, `height()`, `width()`, `buffer_len()`, `to_host()`.\n\n**Why**: `fold_ple_evals` (round 0) produces `DeviceMatrix<EF>` via existing `fold_ple_evals_rotate` calls. The first MLE fold round reads these `Owned` matrices and produces `Arena` matrices. From round 2 onward, all matrices are `Arena`. This avoids modifying `fold_ple_evals` entirely.\n\n### Change 3: Add `FoldArena<T>` type (`crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs`)\n\n```rust\nstruct FoldArena<T> {\n    /// Big buffer(s) backing ArenaMatrix views. Grows by one buffer per\n    /// allocate_bulk call. Older buffers are kept alive because height-1\n    /// ArenaMatrix entries from earlier rounds still point into them.\n    /// Total memory: bounded at ~2× the first round's allocation\n    /// (geometric sum 1 + 1/2 + 1/4 + ... < 2).\n    buffers: Vec<DeviceBuffer<T>>,\n}\n```\n\nMethods:\n- `new() -> Self`\n- `allocate_bulk(total_cells: usize) -> *mut T`: Allocates one `DeviceBuffer` of `total_cells`, pushes to `self.buffers`, returns `as_mut_ptr()`.\n- When `FoldArena` drops, all buffers are freed via their normal `DeviceBuffer::drop`.\n\n### Change 4: Change storage types in `LogupZerocheckGpu`\n\n```rust\n// Before:\nmat_evals_per_trace: Vec<Vec<DeviceMatrix<EF>>>,\nsels_per_trace: Vec<DeviceMatrix<EF>>,\n\n// After:\nmat_evals_per_trace: Vec<Vec<MatrixRef<EF>>>,\nsels_per_trace: Vec<MatrixRef<EF>>,\nfold_arena: FoldArena<EF>,\n```\n\n`fold_ple_evals` (unchanged) populates these with `MatrixRef::Owned(DeviceMatrix<EF>)` values. The MLE fold loop transitions them to `MatrixRef::Arena(ArenaMatrix<EF>)` values from round 1 onward.\n\n### Change 5: Rewrite `batch_fold` in `fold_mle_evals` (`mod.rs:2002–2048`)\n\nReplace per-matrix allocation with arena-based bulk allocation. The closure now accepts `MatrixRef<EF>` inputs:\n\n```rust\nlet batch_fold = |input_mats: Vec<MatrixRef<EF>>,\n                  arena: &mut FoldArena<EF>|\n    -> Result<Vec<MatrixRef<EF>>, LogupZerocheckError>\n{\n    let num_matrices = input_mats.partition_point(|mat| mat.height() > 1);\n\n    // Compute total output cells and per-matrix metadata\n    let mut total_cells = 0usize;\n    let mut offsets = Vec::with_capacity(num_matrices);\n    let mut log_output_heights = Vec::with_capacity(num_matrices);\n    let mut widths_u32 = Vec::with_capacity(num_matrices);\n    let mut max_output_cells = 0usize;\n\n    for mat in input_mats.iter().take(num_matrices) {\n        let h = mat.height() >> 1;\n        let w = mat.width();\n        let cells = h * w;\n        offsets.push(total_cells);\n        total_cells += cells;\n        max_output_cells = max(max_output_cells, cells);\n        log_output_heights.push(h.ilog2() as u8);\n        widths_u32.push(w as u32);\n    }\n\n    if total_cells == 0 {\n        // All matrices already at height 1\n        return Ok(input_mats);\n    }\n\n    // ONE allocation instead of ~1869\n    let arena_ptr = arena.allocate_bulk(total_cells);\n\n    // Build output matrices as arena views\n    let output_arena_mats: Vec<ArenaMatrix<EF>> = offsets.iter().enumerate().map(|(i, &off)| {\n        let h = input_mats[i].height() >> 1;\n        let w = input_mats[i].width();\n        ArenaMatrix::new(unsafe { arena_ptr.add(off) }, h, w)\n    }).collect();\n\n    // Collect pointers for CUDA kernel (reads from input, writes to arena)\n    let input_ptrs: Vec<_> = input_mats.iter().take(num_matrices)\n        .map(|mat| mat.as_ptr()).collect();\n    let output_ptrs: Vec<_> = output_arena_mats.iter()\n        .map(|mat| mat.as_mut_ptr()).collect();\n\n    // Upload and launch (same kernel as before)\n    let d_input_ptrs = input_ptrs.to_device()?;\n    let d_output_ptrs = output_ptrs.to_device()?;\n    let d_log_output_heights = log_output_heights.to_device()?;\n    let d_widths = widths_u32.to_device()?;\n\n    unsafe {\n        batch_fold_mle(\n            &d_input_ptrs, &d_output_ptrs, &d_widths,\n            num_matrices.try_into().unwrap(),\n            &d_log_output_heights,\n            max_output_cells.try_into().unwrap(),\n            r_round,\n        ).map_err(LogupZerocheckError::BatchFoldMle)?;\n    }\n\n    // Build output: arena matrices for folded + carried forward for height-1\n    let mut output_mats: Vec<MatrixRef<EF>> = output_arena_mats\n        .into_iter()\n        .map(MatrixRef::Arena)\n        .collect();\n    // Carry forward unfoldable matrices unchanged (Copy/Clone via MatrixRef)\n    for mat in &input_mats[num_matrices..] {\n        output_mats.push(mat.clone());\n    }\n    Ok(output_mats)\n};\n```\n\n**Allocation reduction**: 1 bulk `cudaMallocAsync` per fold call instead of ~1869 individual calls. Over 20 rounds × 2 groups (mat_evals + sels) = **~40 arena allocs + ~160 auxiliary allocs (pointer arrays) = ~200 total**, down from ~37,380.\n\n### Change 6: Update `sumcheck_polys_batch_eval` consumers (`mod.rs:1536–1922`)\n\nMechanical replacements. All access through `MatrixRef` delegate methods:\n- `mats[i].buffer().as_ptr()` → `mats[i].as_ptr()`\n- `m.buffer().as_ptr().wrapping_add(col * m.height())` → `m.as_ptr().wrapping_add(col * m.height())`\n- `mats[i].height()` → `mats[i].height()` (unchanged)\n- `mats[i].width()` → `mats[i].width()` (unchanged)\n\nThese are ~15 call sites that access buffer pointers.\n\n### Change 7: Update `into_column_openings` (`mod.rs:2098–2172`)\n\nReplace `transport_matrix_d2h_col_major(&mat)` with `MatrixRef::to_host()`:\n\n```rust\nlet host_data = mat.to_host()?;\nlet mat_host = ColMajorMatrix::new(host_data, mat.width());\n```\n\nAt this point all matrices have height=1, so these are tiny copies (width elements each). The `to_host()` method on `MatrixRef` dispatches to either `DeviceMatrix::to_host()` (Owned) or `ArenaMatrix::to_host()` (Arena).\n\n### Change 8: Update `save_memory` accounting (`mod.rs:2067–2075`)\n\nReplace `m.buffer().len() * size_of::<EF>()` with `m.buffer_len() * size_of::<EF>()`. `MatrixRef::buffer_len()` returns `height * width`, matching the current `DeviceBuffer::len()` semantics.\n\n## Invariants\n\n1. **No double-free**: `ArenaMatrix` is `Copy` — it stores a raw pointer but does NOT free memory on drop. Only `FoldArena::drop` frees the big buffers. The `DeviceMatrix` values from `fold_ple_evals` are dropped when they're replaced in the first MLE fold round — their individual `Arc<DeviceBuffer>` handles cleanup normally.\n\n2. **Arena lifetime**: `FoldArena` is a field of `LogupZerocheckGpu` and outlives all `ArenaMatrix` references (stored in `mat_evals_per_trace` and `sels_per_trace` on the same struct). `into_column_openings` consumes `self`, so arena data is read before the struct is dropped. Drop order within a Rust struct is field-declaration order, but since `into_column_openings` takes `self` by value and explicitly processes the matrices before returning, there's no use-after-free regardless of field order.\n\n3. **Arena memory bound**: The arena accumulates buffers over ~20 rounds. Each round's total is approximately half the previous (since fold halves heights). Total arena memory: `S × (1 + 1/2 + 1/4 + ...) < 2S` where S is the first-round total. This matches the current code's memory usage (old `DeviceMatrix` values for height-1 entries are kept alive by `Arc` until replaced).\n\n4. **Correctness**: The CUDA `batch_fold_mle` kernel receives the same pointer arrays as before. The kernel doesn't care whether output pointers come from individual allocations or a single bulk allocation. The fold algorithm is unchanged.\n\n5. **Unfoldable matrices**: Matrices that reach height=1 are carried forward by value via `MatrixRef::clone()`. For `MatrixRef::Owned`, this clones the `Arc`. For `MatrixRef::Arena`, this copies the raw pointer (which is `Copy`). Both cases are correct.\n\n6. **APC 0 behavior**: At APC 0 (~99 AIRs, ~200 matrices), the bulk allocation still applies but the savings are minimal (~3ms). No regression expected since the big buffer allocation amortizes the overhead of 200 individual allocations.\n\n## Measurement Plan\n\n**Benchmark commands** (in powdr repo):\n```bash\nRUST_LOG=info APC=300 openvm-riscv/scripts/run_pairing.sh  # Target metric\nRUST_LOG=info APC=0 openvm-riscv/scripts/run_pairing.sh    # Regression check\n```\n\n**Analysis**:\n```bash\npython3 /home/georg/spec.py results/pairing/apc300/metrics.json after_apc300\npython3 /home/georg/spec.py results/pairing/apc000/metrics.json after_apc000\n```\n\n**Expected results**:\n- MLE Rounds at APC 300: 173ms → 118–133ms (23–32% reduction)\n- STARK excl trace at APC 300: 1322ms → ~1267–1282ms\n- APC 0: No significant change (±5ms noise)\n\n**Nsight verification**:\n- Main-thread `cudaMallocAsync` call count should drop from ~47K to ~10K (eliminating ~37K fold allocs)\n- Main-thread `cudaFreeAsync` call count should drop similarly\n\n## Rollback Criteria\n\nRevert if:\n- MLE Rounds improvement at APC 300 is < 20ms (below noise threshold for a 173ms metric)\n- STARK excl trace improvement at APC 300 is < 30ms\n- APC 0 regresses by > 15ms\n- Correctness failures (proof verification fails at any APC config)\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5259,"cache_read_input_tokens":162209,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":5259},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a8941d98-8943-40ba-ba2b-e8ee56c821e0","uuid":"0d26eb22-e994-4c8f-81f8-a222cbff8743"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01GwmeKTLsybVi51qj7t5Pi4","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt-results/2026-04-14-1200-bulk-alloc-mle-fold-buffers/plan.md has been updated successfully."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a8941d98-8943-40ba-ba2b-e8ee56c821e0","uuid":"e43ea72d-413f-47b7-bc0f-bfbcc88f8e39","timestamp":"2026-04-14T10:20:16.138Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"update","filePath":"/home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt-results/2026-04-14-1200-bulk-alloc-mle-fold-buffers/plan.md","content":"# Plan: Bulk-Allocate MLE Fold Output Buffers\n\n## Goal\n\nEliminate ~37,000 per-round `cudaMallocAsync`/`cudaFreeAsync` calls in MLE sumcheck fold rounds by replacing per-matrix individual allocations with a single bulk allocation per fold group per round. This targets the main-thread allocation overhead visible in nsight (47K mallocs for 38ms + 49K frees for 21ms = 59ms total), which is the primary cause of MLE Rounds reverse-scaling at APC 300 (173ms vs 114ms at APC 0 despite 2x less total work).\n\n**Expected saving**: 40–55ms at APC 300 (MLE Rounds 173ms → ~118–133ms).\n\n**Scope**: Only the MLE fold loop (`fold_mle_evals`, rounds 1..n_max). The one-time `fold_ple_evals` (round 0, ~2500 allocs, ~2ms overhead) is NOT changed — it continues to use `DeviceMatrix<EF>` and is handled by a transitional type in the first MLE fold round.\n\n## Current Code Path\n\n### MLE round loop (`mod.rs:631–648`)\n\nEach round calls:\n1. `sumcheck_polys_batch_eval(round, r[round-1])` — evaluates sumcheck polynomials for all traces\n2. `compute_batch_s_poly(...)` — CPU batching\n3. `fold_mle_evals(round, r_round)` — halves all trace matrix heights via GPU kernel\n\n### `fold_mle_evals` (`mod.rs:2000–2091`)\n\nThe inner `batch_fold` closure (line 2002–2048):\n1. **Counts foldable matrices**: `partition_point(|mat| mat.height() > 1)` → `num_matrices`\n2. **Allocates per-matrix output buffers** (line 2014):\n   ```rust\n   let output_mat = DeviceMatrix::<EF>::with_capacity(output_height, width);\n   ```\n   At APC 300: ~1246 matrices for mat_evals + ~623 for sels = **~1869 individual allocations per round**.\n3. **Collects pointer arrays**, uploads 4 small H2D buffers (input_ptrs, output_ptrs, heights, widths)\n4. **Single `batch_fold_mle` kernel launch** — one kernel folds all matrices\n5. **Appends unfoldable matrices** (height=1) unchanged\n\nOver ~20 MLE rounds: **~37,380 allocs + ~37,380 frees = ~74,760 CUDA memory API calls**.\n\n### Consumers of `mat_evals_per_trace`\n\n`mat_evals_per_trace: Vec<Vec<DeviceMatrix<EF>>>` is accessed at:\n- `sumcheck_polys_batch_eval` (line 1574): `.iter()` for metadata loop — reads `buffer().as_ptr()`, `height()`, `width()` per matrix\n- `sumcheck_polys_batch_eval` (line 1730): `&self.mat_evals_per_trace[meta.trace_idx]` — indexed access for Case B\n- `fold_mle_evals` (line 2051–2066): `std::mem::take`, flatten, fold, restructure\n- `into_column_openings` (line 2107): `std::mem::take`, D2H copy via `transport_matrix_d2h_col_major(&mat)`\n- Memory accounting (line 2069): `.buffer().len()` sum\n\n`sels_per_trace: Vec<DeviceMatrix<EF>>` has the same access patterns in `batch_fold` and `sumcheck_polys_batch_eval`.\n\n### Why it's slow at APC 300\n\nAt APC 300 (623 AIR instances, ~1869 matrices), each fold round allocates ~1869 output buffers via `cudaMallocAsync`. Even though the CUDA pool allocator caches memory (making per-call overhead ~0.8μs), the aggregate over 20 rounds is ~30K alloc calls + ~30K free calls = ~50ms. Combined with CPU-side metadata iteration over 623 traces per round, the total MLE per-round overhead is ~5.5ms at APC 300 vs ~1.5ms at APC 0 (99 AIRs, ~200 matrices per fold).\n\nQuantitative estimate from nsight: the fold accounts for ~(37K/47K) × 38ms allocs + ~(37K/49K) × 21ms frees ≈ 31ms + 16ms = **~47ms** of pure CUDA memory API overhead. Additional savings from reduced memory pool fragmentation bring the total to ~40–55ms.\n\n## Changes\n\n### Change 1: Add `ArenaMatrix<T>` type (`crates/cuda-backend/src/base.rs`)\n\nA lightweight non-owning matrix type that stores a raw pointer + dimensions:\n\n```rust\n#[derive(Clone, Copy)]\npub struct ArenaMatrix<T> {\n    ptr: *mut T,\n    height: usize,\n    width: usize,\n}\n\nunsafe impl<T> Send for ArenaMatrix<T> {}\nunsafe impl<T> Sync for ArenaMatrix<T> {}\n```\n\nMethods: `new(ptr, height, width)`, `as_ptr() -> *const T`, `as_mut_ptr() -> *mut T`, `height()`, `width()`, `buffer_len() -> usize` (returns `height * width`).\n\nAdd a `to_host(&self) -> Result<Vec<T>, MemCopyError>` method that performs a D2H copy of `height * width` elements from `ptr`. This requires calling `cudaMemcpyAsync` directly (import from `openvm_cuda_common::stream::{cudaStreamPerThread, cudaStream_t}` and `openvm_cuda_common::copy::cudaMemcpy_d2h` or equivalent), since there is no backing `DeviceBuffer` to delegate to.\n\nDerive `Copy + Clone` — all fields are `Copy`-safe. This is needed for `extend_from_slice` on unfoldable height-1 matrices in `batch_fold`.\n\n**Why `DeviceMatrixView` is not used**: The existing `DeviceMatrixView<'a, T>` in `base.rs:117-156` has a lifetime parameter `'a`. Using it for `mat_evals_per_trace` would require adding a lifetime to `LogupZerocheckGpu`, which would create a self-referential borrow (the arena and the views are both fields on the same struct). `ArenaMatrix` avoids this by omitting the lifetime — safety is upheld structurally because `FoldArena` (the owner) and `mat_evals_per_trace` (the views) are fields on the same struct, with the struct's `into_column_openings` consuming all views before drop.\n\n### Change 2: Add `MatrixRef<T>` enum (`crates/cuda-backend/src/base.rs`)\n\nTo handle the transition from `DeviceMatrix` (output of `fold_ple_evals`) to `ArenaMatrix` (output of MLE fold), use an enum:\n\n```rust\n#[derive(Clone)]\npub enum MatrixRef<T> {\n    Owned(DeviceMatrix<T>),\n    Arena(ArenaMatrix<T>),\n}\n```\n\nMethods (delegating to inner type): `as_ptr()`, `height()`, `width()`, `buffer_len()`, `to_host()`.\n\n**Why**: `fold_ple_evals` (round 0) produces `DeviceMatrix<EF>` via existing `fold_ple_evals_rotate` calls. The first MLE fold round reads these `Owned` matrices and produces `Arena` matrices. From round 2 onward, all matrices are `Arena`. This avoids modifying `fold_ple_evals` entirely.\n\n### Change 3: Add `FoldArena<T>` type (`crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs`)\n\n```rust\nstruct FoldArena<T> {\n    /// Big buffer(s) backing ArenaMatrix views. Grows by one buffer per\n    /// allocate_bulk call. Older buffers are kept alive because height-1\n    /// ArenaMatrix entries from earlier rounds still point into them.\n    /// Total memory: bounded at ~2× the first round's allocation\n    /// (geometric sum 1 + 1/2 + 1/4 + ... < 2).\n    buffers: Vec<DeviceBuffer<T>>,\n}\n```\n\nMethods:\n- `new() -> Self`\n- `allocate_bulk(total_cells: usize) -> *mut T`: Allocates one `DeviceBuffer` of `total_cells`, pushes to `self.buffers`, returns `as_mut_ptr()`.\n- When `FoldArena` drops, all buffers are freed via their normal `DeviceBuffer::drop`.\n\n### Change 4: Change storage types in `LogupZerocheckGpu`\n\n```rust\n// Before:\nmat_evals_per_trace: Vec<Vec<DeviceMatrix<EF>>>,\nsels_per_trace: Vec<DeviceMatrix<EF>>,\n\n// After:\nmat_evals_per_trace: Vec<Vec<MatrixRef<EF>>>,\nsels_per_trace: Vec<MatrixRef<EF>>,\nfold_arena: FoldArena<EF>,\n```\n\n`fold_ple_evals` (unchanged) populates these with `MatrixRef::Owned(DeviceMatrix<EF>)` values. The MLE fold loop transitions them to `MatrixRef::Arena(ArenaMatrix<EF>)` values from round 1 onward.\n\n### Change 5: Rewrite `batch_fold` in `fold_mle_evals` (`mod.rs:2002–2048`)\n\nReplace per-matrix allocation with arena-based bulk allocation. The closure now accepts `MatrixRef<EF>` inputs:\n\n```rust\nlet batch_fold = |input_mats: Vec<MatrixRef<EF>>,\n                  arena: &mut FoldArena<EF>|\n    -> Result<Vec<MatrixRef<EF>>, LogupZerocheckError>\n{\n    let num_matrices = input_mats.partition_point(|mat| mat.height() > 1);\n\n    // Compute total output cells and per-matrix metadata\n    let mut total_cells = 0usize;\n    let mut offsets = Vec::with_capacity(num_matrices);\n    let mut log_output_heights = Vec::with_capacity(num_matrices);\n    let mut widths_u32 = Vec::with_capacity(num_matrices);\n    let mut max_output_cells = 0usize;\n\n    for mat in input_mats.iter().take(num_matrices) {\n        let h = mat.height() >> 1;\n        let w = mat.width();\n        let cells = h * w;\n        offsets.push(total_cells);\n        total_cells += cells;\n        max_output_cells = max(max_output_cells, cells);\n        log_output_heights.push(h.ilog2() as u8);\n        widths_u32.push(w as u32);\n    }\n\n    if total_cells == 0 {\n        // All matrices already at height 1\n        return Ok(input_mats);\n    }\n\n    // ONE allocation instead of ~1869\n    let arena_ptr = arena.allocate_bulk(total_cells);\n\n    // Build output matrices as arena views\n    let output_arena_mats: Vec<ArenaMatrix<EF>> = offsets.iter().enumerate().map(|(i, &off)| {\n        let h = input_mats[i].height() >> 1;\n        let w = input_mats[i].width();\n        ArenaMatrix::new(unsafe { arena_ptr.add(off) }, h, w)\n    }).collect();\n\n    // Collect pointers for CUDA kernel (reads from input, writes to arena)\n    let input_ptrs: Vec<_> = input_mats.iter().take(num_matrices)\n        .map(|mat| mat.as_ptr()).collect();\n    let output_ptrs: Vec<_> = output_arena_mats.iter()\n        .map(|mat| mat.as_mut_ptr()).collect();\n\n    // Upload and launch (same kernel as before)\n    let d_input_ptrs = input_ptrs.to_device()?;\n    let d_output_ptrs = output_ptrs.to_device()?;\n    let d_log_output_heights = log_output_heights.to_device()?;\n    let d_widths = widths_u32.to_device()?;\n\n    unsafe {\n        batch_fold_mle(\n            &d_input_ptrs, &d_output_ptrs, &d_widths,\n            num_matrices.try_into().unwrap(),\n            &d_log_output_heights,\n            max_output_cells.try_into().unwrap(),\n            r_round,\n        ).map_err(LogupZerocheckError::BatchFoldMle)?;\n    }\n\n    // Build output: arena matrices for folded + carried forward for height-1\n    let mut output_mats: Vec<MatrixRef<EF>> = output_arena_mats\n        .into_iter()\n        .map(MatrixRef::Arena)\n        .collect();\n    // Carry forward unfoldable matrices unchanged (Copy/Clone via MatrixRef)\n    for mat in &input_mats[num_matrices..] {\n        output_mats.push(mat.clone());\n    }\n    Ok(output_mats)\n};\n```\n\n**Allocation reduction**: 1 bulk `cudaMallocAsync` per fold call instead of ~1869 individual calls. Over 20 rounds × 2 groups (mat_evals + sels) = **~40 arena allocs + ~160 auxiliary allocs (pointer arrays) = ~200 total**, down from ~37,380.\n\n### Change 6: Update `sumcheck_polys_batch_eval` consumers (`mod.rs:1536–1922`)\n\nMechanical replacements. All access through `MatrixRef` delegate methods:\n- `mats[i].buffer().as_ptr()` → `mats[i].as_ptr()`\n- `m.buffer().as_ptr().wrapping_add(col * m.height())` → `m.as_ptr().wrapping_add(col * m.height())`\n- `mats[i].height()` → `mats[i].height()` (unchanged)\n- `mats[i].width()` → `mats[i].width()` (unchanged)\n\nThese are ~15 call sites that access buffer pointers.\n\n### Change 7: Update `into_column_openings` (`mod.rs:2098–2172`)\n\nReplace `transport_matrix_d2h_col_major(&mat)` with `MatrixRef::to_host()`:\n\n```rust\nlet host_data = mat.to_host()?;\nlet mat_host = ColMajorMatrix::new(host_data, mat.width());\n```\n\nAt this point all matrices have height=1, so these are tiny copies (width elements each). The `to_host()` method on `MatrixRef` dispatches to either `DeviceMatrix::to_host()` (Owned) or `ArenaMatrix::to_host()` (Arena).\n\n### Change 8: Update `save_memory` accounting (`mod.rs:2067–2075`)\n\nReplace `m.buffer().len() * size_of::<EF>()` with `m.buffer_len() * size_of::<EF>()`. `MatrixRef::buffer_len()` returns `height * width`, matching the current `DeviceBuffer::len()` semantics.\n\n## Invariants\n\n1. **No double-free**: `ArenaMatrix` is `Copy` — it stores a raw pointer but does NOT free memory on drop. Only `FoldArena::drop` frees the big buffers. The `DeviceMatrix` values from `fold_ple_evals` are dropped when they're replaced in the first MLE fold round — their individual `Arc<DeviceBuffer>` handles cleanup normally.\n\n2. **Arena lifetime**: `FoldArena` is a field of `LogupZerocheckGpu` and outlives all `ArenaMatrix` references (stored in `mat_evals_per_trace` and `sels_per_trace` on the same struct). `into_column_openings` consumes `self`, so arena data is read before the struct is dropped. Drop order within a Rust struct is field-declaration order, but since `into_column_openings` takes `self` by value and explicitly processes the matrices before returning, there's no use-after-free regardless of field order.\n\n3. **Arena memory bound**: The arena accumulates buffers over ~20 rounds. Each round's total is approximately half the previous (since fold halves heights). Total arena memory: `S × (1 + 1/2 + 1/4 + ...) < 2S` where S is the first-round total. This matches the current code's memory usage (old `DeviceMatrix` values for height-1 entries are kept alive by `Arc` until replaced).\n\n4. **Correctness**: The CUDA `batch_fold_mle` kernel receives the same pointer arrays as before. The kernel doesn't care whether output pointers come from individual allocations or a single bulk allocation. The fold algorithm is unchanged.\n\n5. **Unfoldable matrices**: Matrices that reach height=1 are carried forward by value via `MatrixRef::clone()`. For `MatrixRef::Owned`, this clones the `Arc`. For `MatrixRef::Arena`, this copies the raw pointer (which is `Copy`). Both cases are correct.\n\n6. **APC 0 behavior**: At APC 0 (~99 AIRs, ~200 matrices), the bulk allocation still applies but the savings are minimal (~3ms). No regression expected since the big buffer allocation amortizes the overhead of 200 individual allocations.\n\n## Measurement Plan\n\n**Benchmark commands** (in powdr repo):\n```bash\nRUST_LOG=info APC=300 openvm-riscv/scripts/run_pairing.sh  # Target metric\nRUST_LOG=info APC=0 openvm-riscv/scripts/run_pairing.sh    # Regression check\n```\n\n**Analysis**:\n```bash\npython3 /home/georg/spec.py results/pairing/apc300/metrics.json after_apc300\npython3 /home/georg/spec.py results/pairing/apc000/metrics.json after_apc000\n```\n\n**Expected results**:\n- MLE Rounds at APC 300: 173ms → 118–133ms (23–32% reduction)\n- STARK excl trace at APC 300: 1322ms → ~1267–1282ms\n- APC 0: No significant change (±5ms noise)\n\n**Nsight verification**:\n- Main-thread `cudaMallocAsync` call count should drop from ~47K to ~10K (eliminating ~37K fold allocs)\n- Main-thread `cudaFreeAsync` call count should drop similarly\n\n## Rollback Criteria\n\nRevert if:\n- MLE Rounds improvement at APC 300 is < 20ms (below noise threshold for a 173ms metric)\n- STARK excl trace improvement at APC 300 is < 30ms\n- APC 0 regresses by > 15ms\n- Correctness failures (proof verification fails at any APC config)\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":2,"oldLines":10,"newStart":2,"newLines":12,"lines":[" "," ## Goal"," ","-Eliminate ~40,000 per-round `cudaMallocAsync`/`cudaFreeAsync` calls in MLE sumcheck fold rounds by replacing per-matrix individual allocations with a single bulk allocation per fold group per round. This targets the main-thread allocation overhead visible in nsight (47K mallocs for 38ms + 49K frees for 21ms = 59ms total), which is the primary cause of MLE Rounds reverse-scaling at APC 300 (173ms vs 114ms at APC 0 despite 2x less total work).","+Eliminate ~37,000 per-round `cudaMallocAsync`/`cudaFreeAsync` calls in MLE sumcheck fold rounds by replacing per-matrix individual allocations with a single bulk allocation per fold group per round. This targets the main-thread allocation overhead visible in nsight (47K mallocs for 38ms + 49K