{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01NjDvYZVxpLmKE7XQPhLmAD","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Plan: Bulk-Allocate MLE Fold Output Buffers\n2\t\n3\t## Goal\n4\t\n5\tEliminate ~37,000 per-round `cudaMallocAsync`/`cudaFreeAsync` calls in MLE sumcheck fold rounds by replacing per-matrix individual allocations with a single bulk allocation per fold group per round. This targets the main-thread allocation overhead visible in nsight (47K mallocs for 38ms + 49K frees for 21ms = 59ms total), which is the primary cause of MLE Rounds reverse-scaling at APC 300 (173ms vs 114ms at APC 0 despite 2x less total work).\n6\t\n7\t**Expected saving**: 40–55ms at APC 300 (MLE Rounds 173ms → ~118–133ms).\n8\t\n9\t**Scope**: Only the MLE fold loop (`fold_mle_evals`, rounds 1..n_max). The one-time `fold_ple_evals` (round 0, ~2500 allocs, ~2ms overhead) is NOT changed — it continues to use `DeviceMatrix<EF>` and is handled by a transitional type in the first MLE fold round.\n10\t\n11\t## Current Code Path\n12\t\n13\t### MLE round loop (`mod.rs:631–648`)\n14\t\n15\tEach round calls:\n16\t1. `sumcheck_polys_batch_eval(round, r[round-1])` — evaluates sumcheck polynomials for all traces\n17\t2. `compute_batch_s_poly(...)` — CPU batching\n18\t3. `fold_mle_evals(round, r_round)` — halves all trace matrix heights via GPU kernel\n19\t\n20\t### `fold_mle_evals` (`mod.rs:2000–2091`)\n21\t\n22\tThe inner `batch_fold` closure (line 2002–2048):\n23\t1. **Counts foldable matrices**: `partition_point(|mat| mat.height() > 1)` → `num_matrices`\n24\t2. **Allocates per-matrix output buffers** (line 2014):\n25\t   ```rust\n26\t   let output_mat = DeviceMatrix::<EF>::with_capacity(output_height, width);\n27\t   ```\n28\t   At APC 300: ~1246 matrices for mat_evals + ~623 for sels = **~1869 individual allocations per round**.\n29\t3. **Collects pointer arrays**, uploads 4 small H2D buffers (input_ptrs, output_ptrs, heights, widths)\n30\t4. **Single `batch_fold_mle` kernel launch** — one kernel folds all matrices\n31\t5. **Appends unfoldable matrices** (height=1) unchanged\n32\t\n33\tOver ~20 MLE rounds: **~37,380 allocs + ~37,380 frees = ~74,760 CUDA memory API calls**.\n34\t\n35\t### Consumers of `mat_evals_per_trace`\n36\t\n37\t`mat_evals_per_trace: Vec<Vec<DeviceMatrix<EF>>>` is accessed at:\n38\t- `sumcheck_polys_batch_eval` (line 1574): `.iter()` for metadata loop — reads `buffer().as_ptr()`, `height()`, `width()` per matrix\n39\t- `sumcheck_polys_batch_eval` (line 1730): `&self.mat_evals_per_trace[meta.trace_idx]` — indexed access for Case B\n40\t- `fold_mle_evals` (line 2051–2066): `std::mem::take`, flatten, fold, restructure\n41\t- `into_column_openings` (line 2107): `std::mem::take`, D2H copy via `transport_matrix_d2h_col_major(&mat)`\n42\t- Memory accounting (line 2069): `.buffer().len()` sum\n43\t\n44\t`sels_per_trace: Vec<DeviceMatrix<EF>>` has the same access patterns in `batch_fold` and `sumcheck_polys_batch_eval`.\n45\t\n46\t### Why it's slow at APC 300\n47\t\n48\tAt APC 300 (623 AIR instances, ~1869 matrices), each fold round allocates ~1869 output buffers via `cudaMallocAsync`. Even though the CUDA pool allocator caches memory (making per-call overhead ~0.8μs), the aggregate over 20 rounds is ~30K alloc calls + ~30K free calls = ~50ms. Combined with CPU-side metadata iteration over 623 traces per round, the total MLE per-round overhead is ~5.5ms at APC 300 vs ~1.5ms at APC 0 (99 AIRs, ~200 matrices per fold).\n49\t\n50\tQuantitative estimate from nsight: the fold accounts for ~(37K/47K) × 38ms allocs + ~(37K/49K) × 21ms frees ≈ 31ms + 16ms = **~47ms** of pure CUDA memory API overhead. Additional savings from reduced memory pool fragmentation bring the total to ~40–55ms.\n51\t\n52\t## Changes\n53\t\n54\t### Change 1: Add `ArenaMatrix<T>` type (`crates/cuda-backend/src/base.rs`)\n55\t\n56\tA lightweight non-owning matrix type that stores a raw pointer + dimensions:\n57\t\n58\t```rust\n59\t#[derive(Clone, Copy)]\n60\tpub struct ArenaMatrix<T> {\n61\t    ptr: *mut T,\n62\t    height: usize,\n63\t    width: usize,\n64\t}\n65\t\n66\tunsafe impl<T> Send for ArenaMatrix<T> {}\n67\tunsafe impl<T> Sync for ArenaMatrix<T> {}\n68\t```\n69\t\n70\tMethods: `new(ptr, height, width)`, `as_ptr() -> *const T`, `as_mut_ptr() -> *mut T`, `height()`, `width()`, `buffer_len() -> usize` (returns `height * width`).\n71\t\n72\tAdd a `to_host(&self) -> Result<Vec<T>, MemCopyError>` method that performs a D2H copy of `height * width` elements from `ptr`. This requires calling `cudaMemcpyAsync` directly (import from `openvm_cuda_common::stream::{cudaStreamPerThread, cudaStream_t}` and `openvm_cuda_common::copy::cudaMemcpy_d2h` or equivalent), since there is no backing `DeviceBuffer` to delegate to.\n73\t\n74\tDerive `Copy + Clone` — all fields are `Copy`-safe. This is needed for `extend_from_slice` on unfoldable height-1 matrices in `batch_fold`.\n75\t\n76\t**Why `DeviceMatrixView` is not used**: The existing `DeviceMatrixView<'a, T>` in `base.rs:117-156` has a lifetime parameter `'a`. Using it for `mat_evals_per_trace` would require adding a lifetime to `LogupZerocheckGpu`, which would create a self-referential borrow (the arena and the views are both fields on the same struct). `ArenaMatrix` avoids this by omitting the lifetime — safety is upheld structurally because `FoldArena` (the owner) and `mat_evals_per_trace` (the views) are fields on the same struct, with the struct's `into_column_openings` consuming all views before drop.\n77\t\n78\t### Change 2: Add `MatrixRef<T>` enum (`crates/cuda-backend/src/base.rs`)\n79\t\n80\tTo handle the transition from `DeviceMatrix` (output of `fold_ple_evals`) to `ArenaMatrix` (output of MLE fold), use an enum:\n81\t\n82\t```rust\n83\t#[derive(Clone)]\n84\tpub enum MatrixRef<T> {\n85\t    Owned(DeviceMatrix<T>),\n86\t    Arena(ArenaMatrix<T>),\n87\t}\n88\t```\n89\t\n90\tMethods (delegating to inner type): `as_ptr()`, `height()`, `width()`, `buffer_len()`, `to_host()`.\n91\t\n92\t**Why**: `fold_ple_evals` (round 0) produces `DeviceMatrix<EF>` via existing `fold_ple_evals_rotate` calls. The first MLE fold round reads these `Owned` matrices and produces `Arena` matrices. From round 2 onward, all matrices are `Arena`. This avoids modifying `fold_ple_evals` entirely.\n93\t\n94\t### Change 3: Add `FoldArena<T>` type (`crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs`)\n95\t\n96\t```rust\n97\tstruct FoldArena<T> {\n98\t    /// Big buffer(s) backing ArenaMatrix views. Grows by one buffer per\n99\t    /// allocate_bulk call. Older buffers are kept alive because height-1\n100\t    /// ArenaMatrix entries from earlier rounds still point into them.\n101\t    /// Total memory: bounded at ~2× the first round's allocation\n102\t    /// (geometric sum 1 + 1/2 + 1/4 + ... < 2).\n103\t    buffers: Vec<DeviceBuffer<T>>,\n104\t}\n105\t```\n106\t\n107\tMethods:\n108\t- `new() -> Self`\n109\t- `allocate_bulk(total_cells: usize) -> *mut T`: Allocates one `DeviceBuffer` of `total_cells`, pushes to `self.buffers`, returns `as_mut_ptr()`.\n110\t- When `FoldArena` drops, all buffers are freed via their normal `DeviceBuffer::drop`.\n111\t\n112\t### Change 4: Change storage types in `LogupZerocheckGpu`\n113\t\n114\t```rust\n115\t// Before:\n116\tmat_evals_per_trace: Vec<Vec<DeviceMatrix<EF>>>,\n117\tsels_per_trace: Vec<DeviceMatrix<EF>>,\n118\t\n119\t// After:\n120\tmat_evals_per_trace: Vec<Vec<MatrixRef<EF>>>,\n121\tsels_per_trace: Vec<MatrixRef<EF>>,\n122\tfold_arena: FoldArena<EF>,\n123\t```\n124\t\n125\t`fold_ple_evals` (unchanged) populates these with `MatrixRef::Owned(DeviceMatrix<EF>)` values. The MLE fold loop transitions them to `MatrixRef::Arena(ArenaMatrix<EF>)` values from round 1 onward.\n126\t\n127\t### Change 5: Rewrite `batch_fold` in `fold_mle_evals` (`mod.rs:2002–2048`)\n128\t\n129\tReplace per-matrix allocation with arena-based bulk allocation. The closure now accepts `MatrixRef<EF>` inputs:\n130\t\n131\t```rust\n132\tlet batch_fold = |input_mats: Vec<MatrixRef<EF>>,\n133\t                  arena: &mut FoldArena<EF>|\n134\t    -> Result<Vec<MatrixRef<EF>>, LogupZerocheckError>\n135\t{\n136\t    let num_matrices = input_mats.partition_point(|mat| mat.height() > 1);\n137\t\n138\t    // Compute total output cells and per-matrix metadata\n139\t    let mut total_cells = 0usize;\n140\t    let mut offsets = Vec::with_capacity(num_matrices);\n141\t    let mut log_output_heights = Vec::with_capacity(num_matrices);\n142\t    let mut widths_u32 = Vec::with_capacity(num_matrices);\n143\t    let mut max_output_cells = 0usize;\n144\t\n145\t    for mat in input_mats.iter().take(num_matrices) {\n146\t        let h = mat.height() >> 1;\n147\t        let w = mat.width();\n148\t        let cells = h * w;\n149\t        offsets.push(total_cells);\n150\t        total_cells += cells;\n151\t        max_output_cells = max(max_output_cells, cells);\n152\t        log_output_heights.push(h.ilog2() as u8);\n153\t        widths_u32.push(w as u32);\n154\t    }\n155\t\n156\t    if total_cells == 0 {\n157\t        // All matrices already at height 1\n158\t        return Ok(input_mats);\n159\t    }\n160\t\n161\t    // ONE allocation instead of ~1869\n162\t    let arena_ptr = arena.allocate_bulk(total_cells);\n163\t\n164\t    // Build output matrices as arena views\n165\t    let output_arena_mats: Vec<ArenaMatrix<EF>> = offsets.iter().enumerate().map(|(i, &off)| {\n166\t        let h = input_mats[i].height() >> 1;\n167\t        let w = input_mats[i].width();\n168\t        ArenaMatrix::new(unsafe { arena_ptr.add(off) }, h, w)\n169\t    }).collect();\n170\t\n171\t    // Collect pointers for CUDA kernel (reads from input, writes to arena)\n172\t    let input_ptrs: Vec<_> = input_mats.iter().take(num_matrices)\n173\t        .map(|mat| mat.as_ptr()).collect();\n174\t    let output_ptrs: Vec<_> = output_arena_mats.iter()\n175\t        .map(|mat| mat.as_mut_ptr()).collect();\n176\t\n177\t    // Upload and launch (same kernel as before)\n178\t    let d_input_ptrs = input_ptrs.to_device()?;\n179\t    let d_output_ptrs = output_ptrs.to_device()?;\n180\t    let d_log_output_heights = log_output_heights.to_device()?;\n181\t    let d_widths = widths_u32.to_device()?;\n182\t\n183\t    unsafe {\n184\t        batch_fold_mle(\n185\t            &d_input_ptrs, &d_output_ptrs, &d_widths,\n186\t            num_matrices.try_into().unwrap(),\n187\t            &d_log_output_heights,\n188\t            max_output_cells.try_into().unwrap(),\n189\t            r_round,\n190\t        ).map_err(LogupZerocheckError::BatchFoldMle)?;\n191\t    }\n192\t\n193\t    // Build output: arena matrices for folded + carried forward for height-1\n194\t    let mut output_mats: Vec<MatrixRef<EF>> = output_arena_mats\n195\t        .into_iter()\n196\t        .map(MatrixRef::Arena)\n197\t        .collect();\n198\t    // Carry forward unfoldable matrices unchanged (Copy/Clone via MatrixRef)\n199\t    for mat in &input_mats[num_matrices..] {\n200\t        output_mats.push(mat.clone());\n201\t    }\n202\t    Ok(output_mats)\n203\t};\n204\t```\n205\t\n206\t**Allocation reduction**: 1 bulk `cudaMallocAsync` per fold call instead of ~1869 individual calls. Over 20 rounds × 2 groups (mat_evals + sels) = **~40 arena allocs + ~160 auxiliary allocs (pointer arrays) = ~200 total**, down from ~37,380.\n207\t\n208\t### Change 6: Update `sumcheck_polys_batch_eval` consumers (`mod.rs:1536–1922`)\n209\t\n210\tMechanical replacements. All access through `MatrixRef` delegate methods:\n211\t- `mats[i].buffer().as_ptr()` → `mats[i].as_ptr()`\n212\t- `m.buffer().as_ptr().wrapping_add(col * m.height())` → `m.as_ptr().wrapping_add(col * m.height())`\n213\t- `mats[i].height()` → `mats[i].height()` (unchanged)\n214\t- `mats[i].width()` → `mats[i].width()` (unchanged)\n215\t\n216\tThese are ~15 call sites that access buffer pointers.\n217\t\n218\t### Change 7: Update `into_column_openings` (`mod.rs:2098–2172`)\n219\t\n220\tReplace `transport_matrix_d2h_col_major(&mat)` with `MatrixRef::to_host()`:\n221\t\n222\t```rust\n223\tlet host_data = mat.to_host()?;\n224\tlet mat_host = ColMajorMatrix::new(host_data, mat.width());\n225\t```\n226\t\n227\tAt this point all matrices have height=1, so these are tiny copies (width elements each). The `to_host()` method on `MatrixRef` dispatches to either `DeviceMatrix::to_host()` (Owned) or `ArenaMatrix::to_host()` (Arena).\n228\t\n229\t### Change 8: Update `save_memory` accounting (`mod.rs:2067–2075`)\n230\t\n231\tReplace `m.buffer().len() * size_of::<EF>()` with `m.buffer_len() * size_of::<EF>()`. `MatrixRef::buffer_len()` returns `height * width`, matching the current `DeviceBuffer::len()` semantics.\n232\t\n233\t## Invariants\n234\t\n235\t1. **No double-free**: `ArenaMatrix` is `Copy` — it stores a raw pointer but does NOT free memory on drop. Only `FoldArena::drop` frees the big buffers. The `DeviceMatrix` values from `fold_ple_evals` are dropped when they're replaced in the first MLE fold round — their individual `Arc<DeviceBuffer>` handles cleanup normally.\n236\t\n237\t2. **Arena lifetime**: `FoldArena` is a field of `LogupZerocheckGpu` and outlives all `ArenaMatrix` references (stored in `mat_evals_per_trace` and `sels_per_trace` on the same struct). `into_column_openings` consumes `self`, so arena data is read before the struct is dropped. Drop order within a Rust struct is field-declaration order, but since `into_column_openings` takes `self` by value and explicitly processes the matrices before returning, there's no use-after-free regardless of field order.\n238\t\n239\t3. **Arena memory bound**: The arena accumulates buffers over ~20 rounds. Each round's total is approximately half the previous (since fold halves heights). Total arena memory: `S × (1 + 1/2 + 1/4 + ...) < 2S` where S is the first-round total. This matches the current code's memory usage (old `DeviceMatrix` values for height-1 entries are kept alive by `Arc` until replaced).\n240\t\n241\t4. **Correctness**: The CUDA `batch_fold_mle` kernel receives the same pointer arrays as before. The kernel doesn't care whether output pointers come from individual allocations or a single bulk allocation. The fold algorithm is unchanged.\n242\t\n243\t5. **Unfoldable matrices**: Matrices that reach height=1 are carried forward by value via `MatrixRef::clone()`. For `MatrixRef::Owned`, this clones the `Arc`. For `MatrixRef::Arena`, this copies the raw pointer (which is `Copy`). Both cases are correct.\n244\t\n245\t6. **APC 0 behavior**: At APC 0 (~99 AIRs, ~200 matrices), the bulk allocation still applies but the savings are minimal (~3ms). No regression expected since the big buffer allocation amortizes the overhead of 200 individual allocations.\n246\t\n247\t## Measurement Plan\n248\t\n249\t**Benchmark commands** (in powdr repo):\n250\t```bash\n251\tRUST_LOG=info APC=300 openvm-riscv/scripts/run_pairing.sh  # Target metric\n252\tRUST_LOG=info APC=0 openvm-riscv/scripts/run_pairing.sh    # Regression check\n253\t```\n254\t\n255\t**Analysis**:\n256\t```bash\n257\tpython3 /home/georg/spec.py results/pairing/apc300/metrics.json after_apc300\n258\tpython3 /home/georg/spec.py results/pairing/apc000/metrics.json after_apc000\n259\t```\n260\t\n261\t**Expected results**:\n262\t- MLE Rounds at APC 300: 173ms → 118–133ms (23–32% reduction)\n263\t- STARK excl trace at APC 300: 1322ms → ~1267–1282ms\n264\t- APC 0: No significant change (±5ms noise)\n265\t\n266\t**Nsight verification**:\n267\t- Main-thread `cudaMallocAsync` call count should drop from ~47K to ~10K (eliminating ~37K fold allocs)\n268\t- Main-thread `cudaFreeAsync` call count should drop similarly\n269\t\n270\t## Rollback Criteria\n271\t\n272\tRevert if:\n273\t- MLE Rounds improvement at APC 300 is < 20ms (below noise threshold for a 173ms metric)\n274\t- STARK excl trace improvement at APC 300 is < 30ms\n275\t- APC 0 regresses by > 15ms\n276\t- Correctness failures (proof verification fails at any APC config)\n277\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_011CzNcHWL7gzwGd3QZwovAf","session_id":"a8941d98-8943-40ba-ba2b-e8ee56c821e0","uuid":"0c48b56a-3e0d-4ab4-b6d2-f87b7cd815fa","timestamp":"2026-04-14T10:20:35.165Z"}
